// graph.mn — Graph machinery (spec 00)
//
// The substrate. One live graph, all variables. Paged storage,
// O(1) chase amortized, epoch-monotonic, trail-backed for rollback.
// Every TVar(id) is a handle into this graph; every observer chases
// via graph_chase; only inference writes.
//
// types.mn owns the vocabulary (NodeKind, GNode, Graph, Mutation,
// GraphRead, GraphWrite). This file owns the mechanism:
// graph_handler (the live state), the SpinePage record (the paged
// storage), chase_node (the read primitive), occurs_in (cycle
// detection), the revert walkers (speculative rollback).
//
// THE PAGED SPINE. A handle's whole row — its union-find cell, its
// AST body, its attached prose, its e-class edge, its narrowing, its
// executable boundary — lives in ONE SpinePage record: band =
// handle / spine_slots, slot = handle % spine_slots. Pages allocate
// WHOLE at band-open (all six columns) and NEVER MOVE — the only
// mutation is an in-place cell write, so a checkpoint carries no
// spine copies: the open-band count is the whole guard, and
// rollback restores the COUNT. Cells and band-table slots
// above a restored count are stale and get overwritten at the next
// mint/band-open — the trail's own logical-length discipline, one
// level up. This shape deletes the doubling copy (the old
// graph_extend_to), the NFree gap-fill, and the seventeen-field
// checkpoint snapshot that moving spines forced: a page that never
// relocates cannot dangle, so the fork-spine class (the boundaries[23]
// stale-spine trap) is unconstructible, and a snapshot of the band
// table stays valid across mints.
//
// The trail keeps the flat-buffer shape — append O(1) amortized,
// rollback O(M) exact, entries above trail_len stale-and-overwritten.
// Mentl's oracle (spec 09) depends on that O(M) guarantee to explore
// hundreds of candidate patches per second.
//
// The per-module overlay family is DELETED (2026-07-25): graph_fork
// never performed anywhere in the tree, so the module partition never
// existed at runtime — every mint registered into one "global" row
// feeding a filter nobody called. Module-of-position reads LIVE through
// NModule span containment (module_path_of_span below), never a
// per-mint side-ledger.
import types

import effects

import strings

import imap

import io

// graph_handler — the substrate handler
// State:
//   spine               — band table: SpinePage records, one per open band
//   spine_open          — count of open bands (cells above are stale)
//   trail               — flat mutation buffer (append forward, walk back on rollback)
//   trail_len           — logical length of trail (entries above are stale)
//   epoch               — bumps on every bind; query observers key (handle, epoch)
//   next                — next fresh handle to allocate
//
// Threaded through the pipeline via ~>. Installed once at compile
// entry. Every downstream pass (infer, lower, query, teach, emit,
// mentl) sees the same live graph through the same effect discipline.
//
// This is the canonical demonstration of Mentl's handler-state pattern:
// state is internal to the handler; callers never see Graph as
// a value; access is only through perform.
//
// Reverse projection over the comment-ref pairs: which comment owners
// reference this node. Linear over the pair list — the pair count is the
// program's authored cross-references, small by construction.
fn comment_ref_owners(ref pairs, referent, i, acc) with Memory + Alloc = if i >= len(pairs) {
  acc
} else {
  match list_index(pairs, i) {
    (o, r) => comment_ref_owners(pairs, referent, i + 1, if r == referent { acc ++ [o] } else { acc }),
  }
}

// ─── The paged spine — six columns, one page record per band ─────────────
// Column absent-contracts (a virgin cell is word 0 — the allocator's
// virginity contract IS the column's): the nodes column is MINT-DENSE
// (every handle below the mint bound was written at its own mint, so reads
// guard by the bound and never see a virgin cell); the program and comments
// columns guard the cell's word face (addr(cell) == 0 — the Cast
// vocabulary's read) and answer the absent value; the canon and narrowing
// columns read 0 as "no edge"; a boundaries cell's 0 IS NoBoundary (nullary
// tag 0) natively. The page is a STRUCTURAL record — mechanism, not a
// branded domain value — so field access unifies (nominal TName refuses the
// open-row field demand), and spine_page's return annotation is the shape's
// one home.
//
// One truth for the band width. 16384 slots sizes the page to the measured
// mint distribution (max 2,305 mints per decl on the wheel, p99 331 — 7×
// headroom for the per-decl banding this page structure carries next).
let spine_slots = 16384

fn spine_band(h) = h / spine_slots

fn spine_slot(h) = h % spine_slots

// The one page read — callers guarantee the band is open (mint-density for
// nodes; a spine_open guard for the sparse columns). A closed-band index
// beyond the table is the loud OOB trap, never a silent default. The return
// annotation is the Intent Boundary that CLOSES the record: an inferred open
// {nodes, ...} receiver computes field offsets over the partial demanded set
// (the trecordopen-wrong-field class — measured here as `.nodes` reading
// offset 0, the boundaries column, before the annotation).
fn spine_page(ref spine, h) -> SpinePage with Memory = list_index(spine, spine_band(h))

// Open bands through band b — the MINT path only: every other write lands
// at an already-minted handle, whose band is open by construction. Returns
// the (possibly grown) table and the new open count; the table grows by
// list_extend_to (rare — once per 16384 mints), the pages themselves never
// move.
fn spine_ensure(own spine, spine_open, b) with Memory + Alloc + ImageAlloc = if b < spine_open {
  (spine, spine_open)
} else {
  // IMAGE family (1) of six (arena 2a-ii, Hβ.perf.per-decl-arena): the
  // spine's pages OUTLIVE every per-decl reset — the bracket classifies
  // the whole growth extent (table + pages) and feeds the census delta.
  image_enter()
  let grown = spine_open_loop(list_extend_to(spine, b + 1), spine_open, b)
  image_exit()
  grown
}

fn spine_open_loop(own spine, opened, b) with Memory + Alloc = if opened > b {
  (spine, opened)
} else {
  let page =
    SpinePage{bind: make_list(spine_slots), boundaries: make_list(spine_slots), canon: make_list(spine_slots), cells: make_list(spine_slots), comments: make_list(spine_slots), dispatch: make_list(spine_slots), installs: make_list(spine_slots), mark: make_list(spine_slots), module: make_list(spine_slots), narrowing: make_list(spine_slots), nodes: make_list(spine_slots), parent: make_list(spine_slots), program: make_list(spine_slots), slots: make_list(spine_slots), spans: make_list(spine_slots)}
  spine_open_loop(list_set(spine, opened, page), opened + 1, b)
}

// In-place cell writes — the page never moves, so no write returns a new
// spine and no handler-state rebind rides a cell store.
fn spine_put_node(ref spine, h, own node) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).nodes, spine_slot(h), node)
  ()
}

// A handle's cell slot — an effect row's cell or its union-find pointer. A
// handle that holds none reads the page's zero word, which is what the
// word-face guard answers for.
fn spine_put_cell(spine, h, slot) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).cells, spine_slot(h), slot)
  ()
}

fn cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, h) = match cell_at(spine, mint_bound, h) {
  CEmpty => false,
  _ => true,
}

// What a handle holds beside its node. A handle past the mint bound holds
// nothing — its band may not be open — and reads as the empty slot it is.
fn cell_at(spine, mint_bound, h) -> CellSlot with Memory =
  if h >= 0 && h < mint_bound { cell_slot_at(spine, h) } else { CEmpty }

fn cell_slot_at(spine, h) -> CellSlot with Memory =
  list_index(spine_page(spine, h).cells, spine_slot(h))

fn spine_put_body(ref spine, h, own body) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).program, spine_slot(h), body)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_comment(ref spine, h, own cell) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).comments, spine_slot(h), cell)
  ()
}

// The module a node was born in, stamped at the mint from the handler's
// live cur_module. Not trail-backed: a rolled-back branch's cells are
// discarded whole, and a re-mint at the same handle restamps — there is no
// state to invert. The column is TOTAL by construction: a mint always runs
// inside some module, because entering one is what parsing a file IS.
fn spine_put_module(ref spine, h, m) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).module, spine_slot(h), m)
  ()
}

// The occurs check's mark on a root: the stamp of the check that reached it.
// A root is always a minted handle, so its band is open.
fn spine_mark_at(spine, h) with Memory = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).mark, spine_slot(h))

fn spine_put_mark(spine, h, stamp) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).mark, spine_slot(h), stamp)
  ()
}

// The module in force, named — the mechanism-layer read for the write
// guard's own reports. It reads THIS instance's state directly rather than
// performing graph_module_current, because an arm's performs resolve OUTER
// of its own install (the wrong-instance class this guard family already
// pays for) and because charging GraphRead into this handler's residual is
// the row leak its comment names.
fn spine_module_name(ref spine, spine_open, m) with Memory + Cast + Alloc = match spine_body_at(spine, spine_open, m) {
  NModule(mm) => mm.path,
  _ => {
    load_i32(0 - 1)
    ""
  },
}

fn spine_put_canon(ref spine, h, to) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).canon, spine_slot(h), to)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_narrowing(ref spine, h, pred) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).narrowing, spine_slot(h), pred)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_boundary(ref spine, h, own boundary) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).boundaries, spine_slot(h), boundary)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_bind(ref spine, h, bh) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).bind, spine_slot(h), bh)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_dispatch(spine, h, dt) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).dispatch, spine_slot(h), dt)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_slots(spine, h, sh) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).slots, spine_slot(h), sh)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_install(spine, h, ish) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).installs, spine_slot(h), ish)
  ()
}

fn spine_put_span(ref spine, h, own span) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, h).spans, spine_slot(h), span)
  ()
}

// The tree edge, written where a node registers: each child learns its
// container. The write is in-place, so a re-registration (a module's second,
// with its parsed decls) writes it again. A LEAF writes nothing: it contains
// no node, so it is no one's parent.
fn spine_put_tree(spine, h, kids) with Memory + Alloc =
  if len(kids) == 0 { () } else { each({ c => spine_put_parent(spine, c, h) }, kids) }

fn spine_put_parent(spine, c, h) with Memory = {
  let _ = list_set(spine_page(spine, c).parent, spine_slot(c), h + 1)
  ()
}

// A node no container registered — a type cell, a root, an orphan — has no
// parent: the column's absent answer.
fn spine_parent_at(spine, spine_open, h) -> Option(Int) with Memory + Alloc = if h < 0 || spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  None
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).parent, spine_slot(h))
  if cell == 0 { None } else { Some(cell - 1) }
}

// The sparse-column reads, each answering its absent-contract for a closed
// band or a virgin cell.
fn spine_body_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory + Cast + Alloc = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  NHole(0)
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).program, spine_slot(h))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { NHole(0) } else { cell }
}

// The birth-span column read — a virgin cell (never parse-indexed) is
// span_zero, the same absent-contract every position consumer already
// treats as "no source site". A negative handle names no node — a bound a
// value row carries with no declaring site (`cellify_rows`) — and reads the
// same absence, as `spine_module_of` already did; indexing it faulted the
// whole compile at the first breach of such a bound.
fn spine_span_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory + Cast + Alloc = if h < 0 || spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  span_zero()
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).spans, spine_slot(h))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { span_zero() } else { cell }
}

fn spine_comment_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory + Alloc + Cast = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  ("", span_zero())
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).comments, spine_slot(h))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { ("", span_zero()) } else { cell }
}

fn spine_canon_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  h
} else {
  let to = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).canon, spine_slot(h))
  if to == 0 { h } else { to }
}

fn spine_narrowing_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  0
} else {
  list_index(spine_page(spine, h).narrowing, spine_slot(h))
}

fn spine_boundary_at(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory = if spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  NoBoundary
} else {
  list_index(spine_page(spine, h).boundaries, spine_slot(h))
}

// Config: a BRANCH CURSOR (the parallel fan's per-thread instance) installs
// over the SHARED spine with a private planned range — the root install is
// bare and the defaults are the empty graph. Handler state records live in
// shared heap; the install chain stays per-instance, correctly private.
handler graph_handler(spine0, spine_open0, span_index0) with spine = spine0, spine_open = spine_open0, trail = [], trail_len = 0, epoch = 0, next = 0, span_index = span_index0, checkpoint_stack = [], comment_refs = [], refs_col = smap_new(), decls_col = [], judged_col = [], phase_col = [], emitfns_col = [], emitfns_idx = [], emitfns_idx_covers = 0, sigs_col = [], holes_pend = [], holes_col = [], cur_module = 0, guard_diags = [], guard_told = [], row_pend = [], row_tally = row_tally_new() {
  graph_chase(handle) => // checkpoint_stack tracks active speculation depth. Each
  // graph_push_checkpoint pushes the current trail_len; each
  // graph_rollback pops. When the stack is empty, writes are at the
  // OUTERMOST commit boundary — perform graph_mutated so the IC fixpoint
  // (the oracle's project_queue_merger re-projects, src/oracle.mn) bumps
  // its epoch only on real graph state changes. Speculative writes
  // (Mentl's oracle exploration between push+rollback) leave the stack
  // non-empty and never emit graph_mutated — otherwise infinite recursion
  // (Mentl would subscribe to her own speculations).
  //
  // ─── Read ops ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    let m0 = heap_mark()
    let node = chase_node(spine, next, handle, 0)
    row_tally_add(row_tally, ByChase, heap_mark() - m0)
    resume(node)
  },
  graph_chase_handle(handle) => resume(chase_handle(spine, next, handle, 0)),
  graph_epoch() => resume(epoch),
  graph_next() => resume(next),
  graph_reason_at(handle) => match graph_node_at(spine, next, handle) {
    GNode(_, r) => resume(r),
  },
  graph_snapshot() => resume(Graph(next, span_index)),
  graph_node_body(handle) => resume(spine_body_at(spine, spine_open, handle)),
  graph_span_of(handle) => resume(spine_span_at(spine, spine_open, handle)),
  graph_module_of(handle) => // The module a node was born in — ONE cell read, never the containment
  // scan that module_path_of_span ran over every node (whose own comment
  // named its "O(1) destiny"; this is that destiny). TOTAL: every node is
  // born inside some module, including stdin's, so there is no absent case
  // to encode. A virgin cell here means a node was minted with no module in
  // force, which is a defect in the caller, and it traps loudly rather than
  // answering a fabricated 0 that every reader downstream would have to
  // re-check (the surrender-fallback this column exists to end).
  {
    if spine_band(handle) >= spine_open { load_i32(0 - 1) } else { 0 }
    // The cell holds the module handle BIASED BY ONE — the Option-niche word
    // this file already uses for mint_high's "0 = unlimited". It is not
    // decoration: a fresh graph's FIRST mint is handle 0, so the first module
    // node IS handle 0, and an unbiased column could not tell "born in module
    // 0" from "never stamped" — measured, the day this column landed, as a
    // trap on handle 1 whose module really was 0.
    let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, handle).module, spine_slot(handle))
    if cell == 0 { load_i32(0 - 1) } else { 0 }
    resume(cell - 1)
  },
  graph_parent_of(handle) => resume(spine_parent_at(spine, spine_open, handle)),
  graph_module_current() => // Enter a module: every subsequent mint is attributed to it. The module
  // node attributes ITSELF, which is what makes the column total without a
  // sentinel — the first stamp has no earlier module to inherit, so it is
  // its own, and the recursion bottoms out at the file the developer named.
  resume(cur_module),
  graph_module_enter(m) => match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(m)) {
    (sp, so) => {
      spine_put_module(sp, m, m + 1)
      resume() with spine = sp, spine_open = so, cur_module = m
    },
  },
  graph_push_checkpoint() => // The checkpoint is the fork's STATE VALUE — but pages never move, so the
  // spine contributes two words (the band-table pointer, for the rare
  // in-fork table growth, and the open count) instead of six spine copies.
  // The append-only logs (trail, span_index, comment_refs, and the row
  // guard's refusals, what it has told, and the unifications it asked for)
  // restore wholesale — a speculative round's told refusal is not told, and
  // the round that re-runs must be refused again; per-cell truth restores by
  // walking the trail backward IN PLACE (pages are stable, so every inverse
  // lands on its live cell — the boundaries[23] stale-spine trap is
  // unconstructible in this representation). The record allocates BEFORE
  // the fork pair's heap_mark, so it survives the region.
  {
    resume(trail_len) with checkpoint_stack = push(
      checkpoint_stack,
      {comment_refs: comment_refs, guard_diags: guard_diags, guard_told: guard_told, next: next, row_pend: row_pend, span_index: span_index, spine: spine, spine_open: spine_open, trail: trail, trail_len: trail_len}
    )
  },
  graph_fresh_ty(reason) => // ─── Write ops ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Each write appends a Mutation to the trail. The trail is the
  // inverse log — on rollback we walk it backwards.
  //
  // A mint is `next`, and nothing else. It used to be the floor of a
  // BANDED PARTITION: a planned range per statement, an overflow target
  // per instance, a private slice per branch cursor, and a loud guard for
  // an exhausted band. Every one of those existed so N candidate cursors
  // could mint into one image without colliding — and the copy fan they
  // served is deleted, so the limit was zero at all eleven installs and
  // the overflow branch was provably unreachable. The apparatus is gone
  // rather than left switched off: a branch nothing can take still costs
  // every mint the test, and a reader the price of believing it matters.
  {
    let m0 = heap_mark()
    match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(next)) {
      (sp, so) => {
        spine_put_node(sp, next, GNode(NFree(epoch), reason))
        spine_put_module(sp, next, cur_module + 1)
        // A WRITE IS OBSERVED BY ONE PARTY: an open speculation, which needs
        // the inverse, or the world, which is told. An inverse nothing open
        // could read is never built (`Hβ.graph.trail-records-what-nothing-can-undo`).
        let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
        let new_trail = if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MFreshNode(next)) } else { trail }
        let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
        if !undo { graph_mutated(epoch) }
        row_tally_add(row_tally, ByMint, heap_mark() - m0)
        resume(next) with spine = sp, spine_open = so, next = next + 1, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
      },
    }
  },
  graph_fresh_row(reason) => match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(next)) {
    (sp, so) => {
      // The node carries the row's reason; the cell carries the row. A reader
      // is shown the cell's read (chase_node), so the node's kind here is only
      // the form an absent cell would read as.
      // The cell goes when the mint is undone: MFreshNode's revert empties
      // it. Before it did, a speculative round's row cell outlived its
      // rollback: the handle number was minted again as a type variable,
      // whose mint writes no cell, so the variable read as a row cell.
      // Self-application was measured there (2026-09-24): the parameter typed
      // as a row, the occurs check never saw an infinite type, and the
      // program compiled and looped.
      spine_put_node(sp, next, GNode(NRowFree(epoch), reason))
      spine_put_cell(sp, next, CRow(row_cell_fresh(epoch)))
      spine_put_module(sp, next, cur_module + 1)
      let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
      let new_trail = if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MFreshNode(next)) } else { trail }
      let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
      if !undo { graph_mutated(epoch) }
      resume(next) with spine = sp, spine_open = so, next = next + 1, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
    },
  },
  graph_fresh_record_row(reason) => match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(next)) {
    (sp, so) => {
      spine_put_node(sp, next, GNode(NRowFree(epoch), reason))
      spine_put_module(sp, next, cur_module + 1)
      let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
      let new_trail = if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MFreshNode(next)) } else { trail }
      let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
      if !undo { graph_mutated(epoch) }
      resume(next) with spine = sp, spine_open = so, next = next + 1, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
    },
  },
  graph_fresh_maybe(reason) => match spine_ensure(spine, spine_open, spine_band(next)) {
    (sp, so) => {
      spine_put_node(sp, next, GNode(NMaybeFree(epoch), reason))
      spine_put_module(sp, next, cur_module + 1)
      let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
      let new_trail = if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MFreshNode(next)) } else { trail }
      let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
      if !undo { graph_mutated(epoch) }
      resume(next) with spine = sp, spine_open = so, next = next + 1, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
    },
  },
  graph_register_node(handle, body) => // Edge-link a node's body into the one graph at its handle — the program
  // COLUMN of the handle's own SpinePage. Every AST node registers here at
  // construction, so any handle resolves to its body live (graph_node_body)
  // — the incremental cursor keys per-module recomputation on the driver's
  // tree scan and warm buckets, never a graph-side ledger. The
  // parse-written program projection, distinct from the mutable
  // union-find type cell in the same page's nodes column — one handle, one
  // page, six columns. In-place cell write: no trail, no state rebind.
  {
    spine_put_body(spine, handle, body)
    spine_put_tree(spine, handle, body_children(body))
    resume()
  },
  graph_attach_comment(handle, text, span) => // The comment column — parse-attached prose in the handle's own page. A
  // comment is graph content (SYNTAX.md Comments): the parser attaches each
  // contiguous // block to the handle of the decl it precedes; the Why
  // engine / formatter / cursor project it live (graph_comment_at). A
  // SEPARATE column, not the node's single Reason slot: graph_bind
  // overwrites that slot at infer with the inference Reason, so a
  // parse-written comment Reason would be clobbered.
  //
  // Blank-separated comment blocks share a following declaration: the
  // nested arm attaches the LATER block first, so an existing entry
  // merges BEHIND the incoming earlier text — source order, one home,
  // the blank line surviving as the paragraph break.
  match spine_comment_at(spine, spine_open, handle) {
    (existing, _) => {
      let merged = if byte_len(existing) > 0 { "{text}\n\n{existing}" } else { text }
      spine_put_comment(spine, handle, (merged, span))
      resume()
    },
  },
  graph_comment_at(handle) => // A virgin cell reads as ("", zero span) — the same absent-contract every
  // column read shares.
  match spine_comment_at(spine, spine_open, handle) {
    (text, _) => resume(text),
  },
  graph_comment_span_at(handle) => match spine_comment_at(spine, spine_open, handle) {
    (_, span) => resume(span),
  },
  graph_clear_comment(handle) => // The take-side of a re-home: restore the cell to the column's own
  // absent form. A separate op because attach MERGES behind existing
  // text (the blank-separated-blocks rule above) — an empty attach
  // would prepend an empty paragraph, never clear.
  {
    spine_put_comment(spine, handle, ("", span_zero()))
    resume()
  },
  graph_attach_comment_ref(owner, referent) => // The comment-REFERENCE edges: a resolved backticked reference is a
  // real edge (owner comment's node -> referent decl's node), drawn at
  // the infer tail once the env is whole. Comments heal under rename
  // because the edge carries the identity and the NAME is a projection;
  // the audit's referenced-by line is the reverse read.
  {
    resume() with comment_refs = comment_refs ++ [(owner, referent)]
  },
  graph_comment_refs_of(referent) => resume(comment_ref_owners(comment_refs, referent, 0, [])),
  graph_canon_set(from, to) => // The e-graph equivalence weave — union-find as a fabric EDGE, a column of
  // the handle's own page beside the program/comments/nodes columns (the TVar
  // union-find precedent). `graph_canon_set(from, to)` draws the equivalence edge `from ≡
  // to`; `graph_canon_at(handle)` reads the one-step pointer (the node's
  // e-class root pointer, or the handle itself if it is its own root). The
  // rule that records the pair guarantees `to` is the cheaper node, so
  // chasing the edge always descends toward the simplest form — egraph_extract
  // (egraph.mn) follows it live. No side-ledger: an already-rooted `from`
  // overwrites its one cell, so saturation never grows an O(N²) pair-list (the
  // OOM the snoc-list ledger caused). No trail: saturation is a `|>` stage
  // between infer and lower, before speculation, so rollback never touches it.
  {
    spine_put_canon(spine, from, to)
    resume()
  },
  graph_canon_at(handle) => // A handle with no edge (absent, or a self-pointer) is its own e-class root —
  // the extracted term. A virgin cell holds 0, and handle 0 is the synthetic
  // Module root (never an expr operand), so a 0 cell reads as "no edge" ⇒
  // return the handle itself.
  {
    resume(spine_canon_at(spine, spine_open, handle))
  },
  graph_narrow_set(handle, pred_handle) => // The refinement-narrowing column — in the handle's page beside canon.
  // graph_narrow_set(handle, pred_handle) records that the path-predicate at
  // bool-node `pred_handle` is proven to hold AT program-point `handle` (§4⑥).
  // graph_narrow_at reads it back — 0 = no narrowing proven (the default for
  // every handle, so an un-narrowed program is byte-identical: lower elides
  // nothing). One cell per handle (idempotent), no side-ledger, no trail — the
  // narrowing is a `|>` stage at infer, read by lower's verify-obligation.
  {
    spine_put_narrowing(spine, handle, pred_handle)
    resume()
  },
  graph_narrow_at(handle) => resume(spine_narrowing_at(spine, spine_open, handle)),
  graph_boundary_at(site_handle) => // The executable-boundary weave: one O(1) read per fanout site, containing
  // its ordered branch edges. Unlike parse-only program/comments and post-
  // infer canon/narrowing, inference writes this weave while the oracle may be
  // under a checkpoint, so every replacement is trailed and rolls back with
  // the type nodes it names.
  {
    resume(spine_boundary_at(spine, spine_open, site_handle))
  },
  graph_boundary_set(site_handle, boundary) => {
    let old_boundary = spine_boundary_at(spine, spine_open, site_handle)
    spine_put_boundary(spine, site_handle, boundary)
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
    let new_trail =
      if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetBoundary(site_handle, old_boundary)) } else { trail }
    let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
    if !undo { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
  },
  graph_bind(handle, ty, reason) => // The guard reads THIS instance's state DIRECTLY: the occurs family is
  // mechanism-layer (state-passed, zero ops), so the check can neither
  // chase a wrong instance (an arm's performs resolve OUTER of its own
  // install — the class that made the write guard read whatever graph
  // the enclosing chain held) nor charge GraphRead into this handler's
  // residual (the row leak that carried the render/guard ops past every
  // bracket into the wheel's own root row).
  {
    let m0 = heap_mark()
    // The stamp is the epoch this write takes. A refusal takes it too, so no
    // two checks ever mark with one stamp.
    let stamp = epoch + 1
    if occurs_in(spine, next, handle, ty, stamp) {
      // THE ONE HOME OF THE OCCURS REFUSAL. Every bind passes here, so this is
      // where an infinite type is refused, whichever unification drew it.
      // STORED, NOT REPORTED: a diag_report here resolves OUTER of this install
      // and sails past any capture bracket installed deeper — which is where
      // speculation installs one, so the refusal the Mycroft retry keys on
      // would never reach it. And STORED UNRENDERED: an arm cannot read the
      // graph its own body reads, so the body that drains it renders it
      // (`report_guard_note`), and the body that judged the fn decides whether
      // the cycle is polymorphic recursion (`poly_teach_notes`) — a question
      // about the fn's own signature, which no single cell's mint answers.
      row_tally_add(row_tally, ByBind, heap_mark() - m0)
      resume() with epoch = stamp, guard_diags = push(
        guard_diags,
        (EOccursCheck(handle, ty, reason_span_or_zero(reason)), "", spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module))
      )
    } else {
      let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
      match old_node {
        GNode(NBound(was), _) => // A BIND OVER A BOUND CELL IS A RE-DERIVATION, and the writer REFUSES
        // it: the cell already carries a proof, and a second write would
        // discard it where a unification would have checked it (`!5`, `5 &&
        // true` and `if 5` all checked clean this way, 2026-09-24 — the
        // operand's cell written with Bool). The refusal is stored like the
        // occurs refusal, and it is an error: a wheel carrying one cannot
        // compile. The grade write (graph_grade) is the one write that
        // legitimately reaches a bound fn cell, and it has its own op.
        {
          row_tally_add(row_tally, ByBind, heap_mark() - m0)
          resume() with epoch = stamp, guard_diags = push(
            guard_diags,
            (EBindOverBound(handle, was, reason, reason_span_or_zero(reason)), "", spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module))
          )
        },
        _ => {
          spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NBound(ty), reason))
          let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
          let new_trail =
            if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetNode(handle, old_node)) } else { trail }
          let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
          if !undo { graph_mutated(stamp) }
          row_tally_add(row_tally, ByBind, heap_mark() - m0)
          resume() with epoch = stamp, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
        },
      }
    }
  },
  graph_grade(handle, params, reason) => // THE GRADE WRITE: only the params' `resolved` slots move. The cell must
  // hold a TFun whose params match the graded ones in every other field —
  // otherwise this write would be a bind over a bound cell wearing a
  // narrower name, and it refuses (stored, like the occurs refusal) rather
  // than write.
  {
    let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
    let graded = match old_node {
      GNode(NBound(TFun{params: was, ret, row}), _) => if grades_only_differ(spine, next, was, params) {
        Some(GNode(NBound(TFun(params, ret, row)), reason))
      } else {
        None
      },
      _ => None,
    }
    match graded {
      Some(node) => {
        spine_put_node(spine, handle, node)
        let new_epoch = epoch + 1
        let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
        let new_trail =
          if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetNode(handle, old_node)) } else { trail }
        let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
        if !undo { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
        resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
      },
      None => resume() with guard_diags = push(
        guard_diags,
        (EInternalInvariant(
          "a grade write reached cell #{int_to_str(handle)}, which does not hold the fn type it grades — the write would have changed more than the params' resolved slots",
          reason_span_or_zero(reason)
        ), "", spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module))
      ),
    }
  },
  graph_bind_hole(handle, reason) => // The productive-under-error poison write (infer.mn:24's law, given its
  // graph op): the node becomes an OBSERVED FAILURE. No occurs check — a hole
  // carries no type to cycle through. Trail-backed like every write, so a
  // speculative checkpoint rolls a poison back with everything else.
  {
    let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
    spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NErrorHole(reason), reason))
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
    let new_trail =
      if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetNode(handle, old_node)) } else { trail }
    let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
    if !undo { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
  },
  graph_bind_absent(handle, reason) => {
    let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
    spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NAbsent, reason))
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
    let new_trail =
      if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetNode(handle, old_node)) } else { trail }
    let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
    if !undo { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
    resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
  },
  graph_row_teach(handle, names, _reason) => // CHARGE: what was performed or constructed here joins the cell's own
  // content, and the read carries forward to every dependent — each one's
  // ceilings checked on the way. One op for every content write; there is no
  // second algebra (no finalize, no replace, no compression), because a
  // cell's read is its least solution and nothing ever re-derives it.
  {
    let m0 = heap_mark()
    let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
    match row_teach_names(
      spine,
      spine_open,
      next,
      handle,
      names,
      row_acc_new(trail, trail_len, row_tally, undo)
    ) {
      acc => {
        row_tally_add(row_tally, ByCharge, heap_mark() - m0)
        if row_acc_quiet(acc) {
          resume()
        } else {
          let new_epoch = epoch + 1
          if !undo && acc.writes > 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
          resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = acc.trail, trail_len = acc.tl, guard_diags = row_acc_diags_into(guard_diags, acc), row_pend = row_acc_pairs_into(row_pend, acc)
        }
      },
    }
  },
  graph_row_link(into, edge, reason) => // A SOURCE edge: `into` holds its source minus the mask, the payload renamed by the edge's renaming.
  // BOTH ENDPOINTS ARE WRITTEN AND TRAILED — the edge on `into`, the reverse
  // edge on `src` — so a rollback restores the subscription with the cell.
  // Both endpoints are ROW cells or the edge is refused: a type variable
  // linked as a row reads as a free root nothing ever binds as a row, and
  // every reader past it sees a type spelled as an effect.
  if !row_sorts_hold(spine, next, [into, edge.src]) {
    resume() with guard_diags = row_diags_join(
      guard_diags,
      [row_sort_refusal(into, edge.src, reason, spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module))]
    )
  } else {
    let m0 = heap_mark()
    let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
    match row_link_cells(spine, spine_open, next, into, edge, row_acc_new(trail, trail_len, row_tally, undo)) {
      acc => {
        row_tally_add(row_tally, row_tally_of_edge(edge), heap_mark() - m0)
        if row_acc_quiet(acc) {
          resume()
        } else {
          let new_epoch = epoch + 1
          if !undo && acc.writes > 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
          resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = acc.trail, trail_len = acc.tl, guard_diags = row_acc_diags_into(guard_diags, acc), row_pend = row_acc_pairs_into(row_pend, acc)
        }
      },
    }
  },
  graph_row_union(a, b, reason) => // UNION — unification proved two positions hold ONE function value, so
  // their rows are one cell: the second root aliases the first, and what
  // either held — content, sources, dependents, bounds — is the root's now.
  // Two ROW cells, as at a link, or the union is refused.
  if !row_sorts_hold(spine, next, [a, b]) {
    resume() with guard_diags = row_diags_join(
      guard_diags,
      [row_sort_refusal(a, b, reason, spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module))]
    )
  } else {
    let m0 = heap_mark()
    let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
    match row_union_cells(spine, spine_open, next, a, b, row_acc_new(trail, trail_len, row_tally, undo)) {
      acc => {
        row_tally_add(row_tally, ByUnion, heap_mark() - m0)
        if row_acc_quiet(acc) {
          resume()
        } else {
          let new_epoch = epoch + 1
          if !undo && acc.writes > 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
          resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = acc.trail, trail_len = acc.tl, guard_diags = row_acc_diags_into(guard_diags, acc), row_pend = row_acc_pairs_into(row_pend, acc)
        }
      },
    }
  },
  graph_row_ceiling(handle, c) => // An upper bound: checked against the read now and at every growth, and
  // carried onto every root the read reaches.
  {
    let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
    match row_ceiling_install(
      spine,
      spine_open,
      next,
      row_root(spine, next, handle, 0),
      c,
      row_acc_new(trail, trail_len, row_tally, undo),
      0
    ) {
      acc => if row_acc_quiet(acc) {
        resume()
      } else {
        let new_epoch = epoch + 1
        if !undo && acc.writes > 0 { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
        resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = acc.trail, trail_len = acc.tl, guard_diags = row_acc_diags_into(guard_diags, acc), row_pend = row_acc_pairs_into(row_pend, acc)
      },
    }
  },
  graph_row_pin(handle, pins) => // A declaration's scalar instances join its frame cell's pins, and the
  // read is carried again so what already arrived is refined too.
  {
    let root = row_root(spine, next, handle, 0)
    if len(pins) == 0 || !cell_slot_held(spine, next, root) {
      resume()
    } else {
      let cell = row_cell_at(spine, next, root)
      let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
      let acc = row_carry(
        spine,
        spine_open,
        next,
        [WakeAll(root)],
        row_put(
          spine,
          next,
          root,
          CRow({...cell, pins: frag_union(cell.pins, pins)}),
          row_acc_new(trail, trail_len, row_tally, undo)
        ),
        row_carry_fuel
      )
      // It wrote the cell, so the world is told, as at every other row write;
      // this arm alone never signalled before.
      let new_epoch = epoch + 1
      if !undo { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
      resume() with epoch = new_epoch, trail = acc.trail, trail_len = acc.tl, guard_diags = row_acc_diags_into(guard_diags, acc), row_pend = row_acc_pairs_into(row_pend, acc)
    }
  },
  graph_row_cell(handle) => resume(row_cell_at(spine, next, handle)),
  graph_row_bytes() => resume(row_tally_read(row_tally)),
  graph_tally_add(t, n) => {
    row_tally_add(row_tally, t, n)
    resume()
  },
  graph_is_row_cell(handle) => resume(cell_slot_held(spine, next, row_root(spine, next, handle, 0))),
  graph_row_pending() => // The instance unifications a mask crossing asked for, handed to the
  // body that can perform them (an arm cannot unify: its performs resolve
  // outer of its own install). Empty — the overwhelming case — resumes
  // without a rebind.
  if len(row_pend) == 0 { resume([]) } else { resume(row_pend) with row_pend = [] },
  graph_bind_record_row(handle, fields, tail, reason) => // The record-row residual bind — a `...R` rest's remaining fields, under
  // its OWN node kind (NRecordRowBound). Binding residuals under NRowBound
  // made the row slot an untagged EffRow-or-Ty union every row walk's
  // catch-all silently crossed. The occurs guard is the TYPE-side check over
  // each field ty (a residual's self-reference is a real infinite type), and
  // the tail's own self-reference is the row-side half of the same check.
  {
    let stamp = epoch + 1
    if cell_slot_held(spine, next, handle) {
      // THE TWO ROW SORTS NEVER MEET AT ONE HANDLE. A record residual bound
      // onto an effect row cell is stored where no read looks — the chase
      // projects the cell — so the bind vanished silently (measured
      // 2026-09-24: a field access's demand minted its rest as an effect
      // cell, and every record met there stayed open). The writer refuses.
      let d = (EInternalInvariant(
        "a record row was bound onto an effect row cell — the rest was minted in the wrong sort",
        reason_span_or_zero(reason)
      ), "", spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, spine_module_of(spine, spine_open, handle)))
      resume() with guard_diags = row_diags_join(guard_diags, [d])
    } else {
      let old_node = graph_node_at(spine, next, handle)
      let dmod = spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module)
      match old_node {
        GNode(NBound(was), _) => // ONE WRITER LAW FOR BOTH SORTS: a cell that already holds a proof is
        // read by unification, never rewritten (graph_bind's own refusal).
        resume() with guard_diags = push(guard_diags, (EBindOverBound(handle, was, reason, reason_span_or_zero(reason)), "", dmod)),
        GNode(NRecordRowBound{fields: had, tail: had_tail}, _) => resume() with guard_diags = push(
          guard_diags,
          (EBindOverBound(handle, record_row_as_type(had, had_tail), reason, reason_span_or_zero(reason)), "", dmod)
        ),
        _ => if occurs_in_fields(spine, next, handle, fields, stamp) || record_row_tail_is(tail, handle) {
          // A residual that reaches its own handle is an INFINITE RECORD — the
          // row would have to contain itself — and it refuses exactly as the
          // type sort does: an `EOccursCheck` stored for the body to render,
          // the would-be binding shown as the record it would have made. It
          // resumed silently until 2026-09-24, and the program typed the two
          // sides of the meet as two different records
          // (tests/syntax/record-row-occurs).
          resume() with epoch = stamp, guard_diags = push(
            guard_diags,
            (EOccursCheck(handle, record_row_as_type(fields, tail), reason_span_or_zero(reason)), "", dmod)
          )
        } else {
          // The TAIL rides from the writer that knew it (types.mn RecordRowTail):
          // proven or continuing. It is never re-derived here, because the
          // fields alone cannot say which produced them.
          spine_put_node(spine, handle, GNode(NRecordRowBound(fields, tail), reason))
          let undo = len(checkpoint_stack) > 0
          let new_trail =
            if undo { trail_append(trail, trail_len, MSetNode(handle, old_node)) } else { trail }
          let new_len = if undo { trail_len + 1 } else { trail_len }
          if !undo { graph_mutated(stamp) }
          resume() with epoch = stamp, trail = new_trail, trail_len = new_len
        },
      }
    }
  },
  graph_index_span(span, handle) => // ─── Rollback — the oracle's undo ────────────────────────────────
  // Walk the trail backwards from trail_len down to target. Each
  // mutation has a precise inverse, applied IN PLACE (pages never move):
  //   MSetNode(h, old) → restore the nodes cell at h to old
  //   MSetRow(h, old)  → restore the cells column at h to old
  //   MSetBoundary(h, old) -> restore the boundaries cell at h
  //   MFreshNode(h)    → decrement next (un-allocate)
  // Reset trail_len = target; entries above are stale and get
  // overwritten on next append — no slicing, no allocation. Epoch is
  // NOT rolled back — it stays monotonic so query observers keyed on
  // (handle, epoch) correctly see invalidation without reordering.
  {
    // ONE write, TWO projections: the ordered (span, handle) log serves
    // the containment scans (address resolution walks span -> handle);
    // the spans spine column serves the by-handle O(1) read
    // (graph_span_of). An in-place cell store rebinds no state.
    spine_put_span(spine, handle, span)
    resume() with span_index = push(span_index, (span, handle))
  },
  graph_ref_note(name, ref_handle) => // The reverse-edge column's write:
  // one O(1) prepend into the smap bucket per reference (the multi-map
  // form — smap_get_all collects every entry). NOT trail-backed: a
  // reference note is append-only telemetry of the judgment's own walk,
  // and a rolled-back speculative branch's notes are dead spans the
  // read-side zero-skip/dedup already drops (the same discipline the
  // whole-graph scan applied to judgment re-mints).
  resume() with refs_col = smap_add(refs_col, name, ref_handle),
  graph_refs_at(name) => resume(smap_get_all(refs_col, name)),
  graph_decl_note(decl_handle) => // The declarations column: every declaring node, noted ONCE — at its
  // birth in the parser (`nstmt`), where a statement comes into being exactly
  // once. It was noted at JUDGMENT, where a speculative round judges a decl
  // again, so every reader deduplicated the whole column, an index-threaded
  // O(n²) walk per read compensating for the writer standing in the wrong place.
  resume() with decls_col = push(decls_col, decl_handle),
  graph_decls_at() => resume(decls_col),
  graph_judged_note(cost) => // The decls column's cost sibling — the same append-only telemetry
  // contract (not trail-backed; one note per judged decl).
  resume() with judged_col = push(judged_col, cost),
  graph_judged_at() => resume(judged_col),
  graph_phase_note(cost) => // The judged column's whole-program sibling: one note per phase of the
  // judgment, the same append-only telemetry contract.
  resume() with phase_col = push(phase_col, cost),
  graph_phases_at() => resume(phase_col),
  graph_hole_note(h) => // The value-holes column's pending half: lower's authored-hole arm is
  // the one writer (value-position is a lowering decision); the pair
  // lands at the next entry drain.
  resume() with holes_pend = push(holes_pend, h),
  graph_hole_drain(entry_name) => // Empty pending — the overwhelming case — resumes without a state
  // rebind, so the per-stmt drain costs nothing when no hole is waiting.
  if len(holes_pend) == 0 {
    resume()
  } else {
    resume() with holes_col = hole_pairs_into(holes_pend, entry_name, holes_col), holes_pend = []
  },
  graph_guard_drain() => // The write guard's stored refusals, handed to the body that can narrate
  // them. Empty — the overwhelming case — resumes without a state rebind,
  // so the per-judgment drain costs nothing when no bind refused. A refusal
  // is one fact however many growths re-detect it — a ceiling carried onto
  // several roots, a read recomputed after the last drain — so what has
  // been told is kept, and only what is new is handed on.
  if len(guard_diags) == 0 {
    resume([])
  } else {
    let fresh = filter({ d => !row_diag_held(guard_told, d) }, guard_diags)
    resume(fresh) with guard_diags = [], guard_told = guard_told ++ fresh
  },
  graph_hole_unnote(h) => resume() with holes_pend = holes_pend_without(holes_pend, h),
  graph_holes_at() => resume(holes_col),
  graph_bind_note(use_handle, bh) => // The bind-home column (lowering-is-a-column
  // step i): an in-place spine cell store at the USE node's handle — no
  // state rebind, non-trailed (lowering runs post-judgment; a re-lower
  // overwrites with the same verdict).
  {
    spine_put_bind(spine, use_handle, bh)
    resume()
  },
  graph_dispatch_note(h, dt) => // The dispatch-tier column (lowering-is-a-column step i): the
  // perform/wasi fork's arms note the chosen tier — same in-place,
  // non-trailed contract as the bind note (lowering runs post-judgment).
  {
    spine_put_dispatch(spine, h, dt)
    resume()
  },
  graph_state_slots_note(h, sh) => {
    spine_put_slots(spine, h, sh)
    resume()
  },
  graph_install_note(h, ish) => {
    spine_put_install(spine, h, ish)
    resume()
  },
  graph_emitfn_note(entry) => // The emittable-fn
  // enumeration (the no-mint resolution): one append per synthesized-fn
  // construction, in lower's deterministic walk order. Non-trailed by
  // the decls contract; the step-iii reader filters by the reach set.
  resume() with emitfns_col = push(emitfns_col, entry),
  graph_emitfn_at(fn_name) => // The enumeration's by-name O(1) read — the identity projection of the
  // ordered column, MATERIALIZED ON DEMAND (the note path stays one push;
  // piggybacking the index there measured +94MB of hot-arm state
  // rebinds). The coverage is the COLUMN LENGTH the index was folded
  // from, not a boolean: an equal length means the projection is whole,
  // a shorter one names exactly the suffix to fold in, so interleaved
  // notes and reads cost O(new entries) total across a run and never a
  // rebuild. A latched flag went stale the moment a note followed a read
  // — which is precisely what the emit-time worklist does
  // (Hβ.lower.lowering-is-a-column).
  //
  // THE ARM HAS A CALLER, and this comment said it had none. It read "the
  // arm has NO call sites yet: the enumeration is step (i) vocabulary,
  // dual-written and zero-read" — written when that was true and left
  // standing after `emitted_sig_of_symbol` (backends/wasm.mn) became its
  // reader, which is the whole emitted-ABI path. A comment claiming zero
  // readers is a claim ABOUT THE GRAPH, and the graph refutes it: that is
  // the confession SYNTAX §«What a comment TRENDS TO» describes, and the
  // projection it confesses is a call-site count the medium can already
  // walk. It cost this session a wrong first read of which structures
  // were live.
  //
  // What the reader gets is the multi-map's newest-first bucket, and the
  // relocation's worklist resolves through it without the quadratic
  // column scan the floor dig measured at a 106s m3 leg.
  //
  // The table stays UNALLOCATED until a read demands it, and that is not
  // hygiene: `graph_handler` installs once per judged decl (the layer
  // sweep's spawn, infer.mn), so a `smap_new()` in its state init is a
  // per-decl 4096-word table — measured at +100MB on the m3 leg's peak,
  // caught by the cost ratchet. Zero coverage means the field is still
  // the empty `[]` that costs nothing.
  if len(emitfns_col) == 0 {
    resume([])
  } else if emitfns_idx_covers == len(emitfns_col) {
    resume(smap_get_all(emitfns_idx, fn_name))
  } else {
    let seed = if emitfns_idx_covers == 0 { smap_new() } else { emitfns_idx }
    let grown = emitfns_index_build(emitfns_col, seed, emitfns_idx_covers)
    resume(smap_get_all(grown, fn_name)) with emitfns_idx = grown, emitfns_idx_covers = len(emitfns_col)
  },
  graph_sigs_publish(pairs) => // THE SIGNATURE COLUMN's one write. The whole table lands in a single
  // rebind at the emit settling point, so no reader can observe a partial
  // one — the property N per-symbol notes could not give.
  resume() with sigs_col = smap_build(pairs),
  graph_sig_at(name) => // Unpublished is the empty `[]` that costs nothing (the emitfns_idx
  // discipline: graph_handler installs per speculative branch, so a
  // smap_new() in the state init would be a 4096-word table per branch).
  // Every read before the publish — every non-emit route, `check`,
  // `query`, the judgment's own — answers None and takes the word floor.
  if len(sigs_col) == 0 { resume(None) } else { resume(smap_get(sigs_col, name)) },
  graph_occurs_in(handle, ty) => // The marks this check leaves are graph state, so the check takes an
  // epoch like any write.
  {
    let stamp = epoch + 1
    let occ = occurs_in(spine, next, handle, ty, stamp)
    resume(occ) with epoch = stamp
  },
  graph_rollback(target_idx) => {
    let n = len(checkpoint_stack)
    if n == 0 {
      // A rollback with no speculation open has nothing to undo: an inverse
      // is recorded only while a checkpoint is open, so there is no entry to
      // walk, and this is a caller that rolled back without pushing — or
      // twice. Refused, stored like every writer refusal. (The unpaired
      // walker that undid writes nobody had fenced is deleted with the
      // entries it walked.)
      resume() with guard_diags = push(
        guard_diags,
        (EInternalInvariant(
          "a rollback reached the graph with no checkpoint open — it was never pushed, or was already rolled back or committed",
          span_zero()
        ), "", spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module))
      )
    } else {
      // Walk the CURRENT trail backward, undoing each write in place into
      // the fork-time table. Backward order makes the oldest trailed
      // old-value win per slot; an entry whose band opened only during the
      // fork is skipped (its page dies with the region). Then every
      // wholesale field — including the two spine words: the band table
      // (for the rare in-fork table growth) and the open count, which
      // closes in-fork bands by COUNT (their table cells are stale above
      // it and get overwritten at the next band-open).
      let cp = last(checkpoint_stack)
      revert_trail_into(cp.spine, cp.spine_open, trail, trail_len, target_idx)
      // drop_last, never slice: the pop runs BEFORE the caller's
      // heap_reset, so a slice node minted here dies with the
      // region and the next fork walks a zeroed cell. On the
      // always-snoc stack drop_last is the parent READ — the pop
      // allocates nothing and the surviving value is pre-fork.
      resume() with spine = cp.spine, spine_open = cp.spine_open, next = cp.next, trail = cp.trail, trail_len = target_idx, span_index = cp.span_index, comment_refs = cp.comment_refs, guard_diags = cp.guard_diags, guard_told = cp.guard_told, row_pend = cp.row_pend, checkpoint_stack = drop_last(checkpoint_stack)
    }
  },
  graph_commit_checkpoint(target_idx) => // The ACCEPT half of speculation: drop the newest checkpoint record
  // WITHOUT restoring — every write since the push stands as truth. Under an
  // enclosing checkpoint the entries stay, because its rollback still walks
  // them. When this was the OUTERMOST one, nothing can read them any more:
  // the trail empties, and the world is told once that what the speculation
  // wrote is now its truth — those writes were fenced from the signal while
  // they were only possible. An empty stack is a commit with nothing pushed,
  // refused like the unpaired rollback.
  if len(checkpoint_stack) == 0 {
    resume() with guard_diags = push(
      guard_diags,
      (EInternalInvariant(
        "a commit reached the graph with no checkpoint open — it was never pushed, or was already rolled back or committed",
        span_zero()
      ), "", spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, cur_module))
    )
  } else if len(checkpoint_stack) == 1 {
    let new_epoch = epoch + 1
    if trail_len > target_idx { graph_mutated(new_epoch) }
    resume() with checkpoint_stack = drop_last(checkpoint_stack), epoch = new_epoch, trail_len = 0
  } else {
    resume() with checkpoint_stack = drop_last(checkpoint_stack)
  },
}

// Acknowledges a mutation and drops it — and today that is EVERY handler
// of `graph_mutated` in the tree, which makes the effect write-only.
//
// This block used to say subscribers "are a handler-swap surface (the IC
// loop's project_queue_merger re-projects on the bumped epoch, MV-LSP.push
// streams diagnostics) … the IC loop swaps it for the oracle's
// re-projection." Measured 2026-09-19: that handler contained ZERO
// `graph_mutated` arms, and the only other arm in the tree is `lsp_adapter`'s,
// which discards both arguments exactly as this one does. The comment
// described an architecture the artifact does not have, which is the one
// thing a comment must never do — and 2026-09-21 finished the sentence: the
// whole ranked-queue oracle it named is DELETED, so those names are prose
// here now and carry no backticks.
//
// WHAT IS ACTUALLY THROWN AWAY, and why it matters more than a dead event:
// the write arms perform `graph_mutated(new_epoch, MSetNode(handle, old_node))`
// — handle AND prior value, at nine sites, guarded so speculation never emits.
// That payload is a DELTA between two images: exactly what an incremental
// re-projection, a board comparing two pins, and a first-divergence question
// each need. The queue merger instead POLLED `graph_epoch()` and re-projected
// the WHOLE queue whenever it moved, collapsing a per-handle cone to one
// boolean — and it is deleted now, so the delta has no consumer at all rather
// than a coarse one. It is still computed (rollback needs it), still performed
// across an effect boundary, and still discarded by everyone.
//
// RETIREMENT: `Hβ.graph.mutation-delta-is-write-only`. This sink stays — a
// chain with no subscriber must still be able to run — but it stops being the
// ONLY reader when the cone lands. Until then the honest name for this handler
// is what it does: a floor, not a seam.
handler mutate_sink {
  graph_mutated(_epoch) => resume(),
}

// Pure graph operations — the mechanism
// Internal machinery the handler delegates to. All pure; all
// deterministic; no effects.
//
// Follow NBound / NRowBound links until terminal. Amortized O(1) —
// chains shallow in practice, depth-limited for cycle safety.
fn chase_node(ref spine, mint_bound, handle, depth) with !Mutate = if depth > 100 {
  GNode(NErrorHole(Inferred("chase depth exceeded")), Fresh(handle))
} else {
  match cell_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
    CRow(cell) => // An effect row cell PROJECTS: its read, one hop, never a walk — the
    // content was carried forward at the write. A cell nothing has charged
    // reads as the free row it is. The reason is the node's.
    match graph_node_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
      GNode(_, reason) => GNode(row_cell_projection(spine, mint_bound, handle, cell), reason),
    },
    CRowAlias(to) => chase_node(spine, mint_bound, to, depth + 1),
    CEmpty => match graph_node_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
      GNode(kind, reason) => match kind {
        NBound(ty) => match ty {
          TVar(next) => chase_node(spine, mint_bound, next, depth + 1),
          _ => GNode(kind, reason),
        },
        _ => GNode(kind, reason),
      },
    },
  }
}

// chase_handle — union-find `find`: follow the TVar chain to the
// canonical ROOT handle (the integer), not the node. unify unions the
// roots; binding a surface alias instead strands the canonical var
// free. Mirror of chase_node's NBound(TVar) arm, returning the handle.
// The value-holes drain's pairing fold: every pending hole pairs with the
// draining entry's name, appended onto the column.
fn hole_pairs_into(hs, name: String, acc) = fold(acc, (a, h) => push(a, (h, name)), hs)

// The emitfn index's extension — the column's UNCOVERED SUFFIX folded
// into the by-name smap. `i` is the coverage the caller already holds,
// so the first read folds the whole column and a read after later notes
// folds only what those notes added.
fn emitfns_index_build(col, idx, i) = if i >= len(col) {
  idx
} else {
  {
    let e = list_index(col, i)
    emitfns_index_build(col, smap_add(idx, e.name, e), i + 1)
  }
}

// The unnote's filter — the pipe-consumed hole leaves the pending buffer.
fn holes_pend_without(hs, h) = fold([], (a, x) => if x == h { a } else { push(a, x) }, hs)

fn chase_handle(ref spine, mint_bound, handle, depth) with !Mutate = if depth > 100 {
  handle
} else {
  match cell_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
    CRowAlias(to) => // Union-find's find at the row sort: an alias is one hop to its root.
    chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, to, depth + 1),
    CRow(_) => handle,
    CEmpty => match graph_node_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
      GNode(kind, _) => match kind {
        NBound(ty) => match ty {
          TVar(next) => chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, next, depth + 1),
          _ => handle,
        },
        _ => handle,
      },
    },
  }
}

// ─── THE EFFECT ROW CELL — the mechanism ─────────────────────────────────
//
// A row is a least solution. A cell's content is what was charged into it
// directly, joined with each source's read across that edge's mask, carried
// FORWARD along the reverse edges at the write — so a read is one hop and no
// reader folds anything. An upper bound is a CEILING: it checks the read,
// and it is carried onto every root the read reaches along a path that is
// not an instantiation — the judgment's own cells, never across into a
// generalized declaration. That is what lands a caller's bound on the copy
// that caller's instantiation owns, where the copy carries it onward to the
// next caller, and never on the declaration every caller shares.
//
// Everything below is state-passed and op-free, and walks structurally
// rather than through the Iterate vocabulary: it runs inside the graph
// handler's own arms, whose performs would resolve outer of this install.
// A write hands back the trail it extended and whatever it could not raise
// itself — a bound refused, an instance unification asked for — for the
// body to narrate and perform.
//
// What a carry hands back: the refusals and unifications it noted, how many
// cells it wrote, the trail it extended when a speculation is open to need
// the inverses (`undo`) — and the tally its wakes' heap is added into.
//
// THE CHANGE SIGNAL IS THE COUNT, never the trail's length. The arms read
// `writes` to decide whether the world saw a change; they used to read
// `tl == trail_len`, which made an undo log nothing could read the only
// evidence that a write happened, so every write outside speculation had to
// be trailed to be seen (`Hβ.graph.trail-records-what-nothing-can-undo`).
type RowAcc = {diags: [(DiagKind, String, String)], pairs: [(EffName, EffName)], tally: [Int], tl: Int, trail: [Mutation], undo: Bool, writes: Int}

// The belt, not the semantics (the HANG law): every write is monotone over a
// finite lattice, so a carry settles; a runaway stops here, loudly.
let row_carry_fuel = 1000000

fn row_acc_new(trail, tl, tally, undo) =
  RowAcc{diags: [], pairs: [], tally: tally, tl: tl, trail: trail, undo: undo, writes: 0}

// A carry the world never saw: no cell written, nothing refused, nothing
// asked of the body.
fn row_acc_quiet(acc: RowAcc) = acc.writes == 0 && len(acc.diags) == 0 && len(acc.pairs) == 0

// THE ROWS' COST IS A TALLY WRITTEN IN PLACE: one flat buffer per graph
// handler, allocated at its install, each slot named by the kind of write or
// wake it counts. The instrument that first split this cost rebuilt a record
// at every wake and every write, so it spent heap inside the very loop it
// measured — the diagnostics ledger's lesson (integer tallies, never a list
// or record rebuilt per event) read one layer over. `RowBytes` exists only
// where it is read.
fn row_tally_slot(t) = match t {
  ByInst => 0,
  ByLink => 1,
  ByCharge => 2,
  ByUnion => 3,
  ByWhole => 4,
  ByDelta => 5,
  ByEdge => 6,
  ByReread => 7,
  ByClone => 8,
  ByScheme => 9,
  ByChase => 10,
  ByBind => 11,
  ByMint => 12,
  ByOwnership => 13,
  ByNarrowing => 14,
}

fn row_tally_new() = list_filled(15, 0)

fn row_tally_add(tally, t, n) = {
  let slot = row_tally_slot(t)
  list_set(tally, slot, tally[slot] + n)
}

fn row_tally_of_wake(wake) = match wake {
  WakeAll{} => ByWhole,
  WakeDelta{} => ByDelta,
  WakeEdge{} => ByEdge,
  WakeFrom{} => ByReread,
}

fn row_tally_of_edge(e: RowEdge) = if e.inst { ByInst } else { ByLink }

fn row_tally_read(tally) =
  RowBytes{bind: tally[row_tally_slot(ByBind)], carry: CarryBytes{all: tally[row_tally_slot(ByWhole)], delta: tally[row_tally_slot(ByDelta)], edge: tally[row_tally_slot(ByEdge)], from: tally[row_tally_slot(ByReread)]}, chase: tally[row_tally_slot(ByChase)], clone: tally[row_tally_slot(ByClone)], inst: tally[row_tally_slot(ByInst)], link: tally[row_tally_slot(ByLink)], mint: tally[row_tally_slot(ByMint)], narrow: tally[row_tally_slot(ByNarrowing)], owner: tally[row_tally_slot(ByOwnership)], scheme: tally[row_tally_slot(ByScheme)], teach: tally[row_tally_slot(ByCharge)], union: tally[row_tally_slot(ByUnion)]}

// What the rows wrote between two readings of the running total.
fn row_bytes_since(now: RowBytes, then: RowBytes) = {
  let c = now.carry
  let d = then.carry
  RowBytes{bind: now.bind - then.bind, carry: CarryBytes{all: c.all - d.all, delta: c.delta - d.delta, edge: c.edge - d.edge, from: c.from - d.from}, chase: now.chase - then.chase, clone: now.clone - then.clone, inst: now.inst - then.inst, link: now.link - then.link, mint: now.mint - then.mint, narrow: now.narrow - then.narrow, owner: now.owner - then.owner, scheme: now.scheme - then.scheme, teach: now.teach - then.teach, union: now.union - then.union}
}

// The rows' heap as one line: each kind of write, then what the carries they
// set off spent per kind of wake, then the type sort's two costs, the graph's
// three arms, and the two passes after a body.
fn row_bytes_line(rb: RowBytes) = {
  let c = rb.carry
  "instantiation {int_to_str(rb.inst)} · link {int_to_str(rb.link)} · charge {int_to_str(rb.teach)} · union {int_to_str(rb.union)}; carried: edge {int_to_str(c.edge)} · delta {int_to_str(c.delta)} · re-read {int_to_str(c.from)} · whole {int_to_str(c.all)}; types: clone {int_to_str(rb.clone)} · scheme {int_to_str(rb.scheme)}; graph: chase {int_to_str(rb.chase)} · bind {int_to_str(rb.bind)} · mint {int_to_str(rb.mint)}; passes: ownership {int_to_str(rb.owner)} · narrowing {int_to_str(rb.narrow)}"
}

// A bound checked at several cells refuses once: the same triple joins the
// held set at most once.
fn row_acc_diags_into(held, acc: RowAcc) = row_diags_join(held, acc.diags)

fn row_diags_join(held, diags) = match diags {
  [] => held,
  [d, ...rest] => row_diags_join(if row_diag_held(held, d) { held } else { push(held, d) }, rest),
}

// Every handle a row write names is a ROW cell — the sort the writer is
// drawing between.
fn row_sorts_hold(spine, mint_bound, hs) =
  all({ h => cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, row_root(spine, mint_bound, h, 0)) }, hs)

// The refusal a row write makes when one of its two ends is not a row. The
// render names both ends by handle, so `mentl <file> chain #h` walks straight
// to whichever is the stray.
fn row_sort_refusal(a, b, reason, dmod) = (EInternalInvariant(
  "a row write joined #{int_to_str(a)} and #{int_to_str(b)}, and one of them is not a row cell — a type stood where an effect row belongs",
  reason_span_or_zero(reason)
), "", dmod)

fn row_diag_held(held: [(DiagKind, String, String)], d) = match held {
  [] => false,
  [x, ...rest] => if x == d { true } else { row_diag_held(rest, d) },
}

fn row_acc_pairs_into(held, acc: RowAcc) =
  if len(acc.pairs) == 0 { held } else { held ++ acc.pairs }

fn row_acc_with_pairs(acc: RowAcc, pairs) = ({...acc, pairs: pairs})

fn row_acc_with_diag(acc: RowAcc, d) =
  if row_diag_held(acc.diags, d) { acc } else { {...acc, diags: push(acc.diags, d)} }

fn row_cell_fresh(born) =
  RowCell{born: born, ceilings: [], charged: [], copies: [], deps: [], edges: [], frees: [], full: [], keyed: [], pins: []}

// MEMBERSHIP IS READ FROM THE LAST ELEMENT BACK, in every walk below. A
// cell's lists grow by `push`, so each is a snoc chain, where `last` and
// `drop_last` read a node where it stands and the walk allocates nothing.
// Matching `[x, ...rest]` from the front minted a slice per element and
// re-walked the chain from its end to reach it, and the in-order reductions
// flatten the chain before their first element: a membership question asked
// at every link of a large frame copied its whole path list each time.
fn row_int_held(xs: [Int], x) = len(xs) > 0 && (last(xs) == x || row_int_held(drop_last(xs), x))

// Find at the row sort — one alias hop at a time.
fn row_root(spine, mint_bound, handle, depth) with Memory + Alloc = if depth > 100 {
  handle
} else {
  match cell_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) {
    CRowAlias(to) => row_root(spine, mint_bound, to, depth + 1),
    CRow(_) => handle,
    CEmpty => handle,
  }
}

// The cell at a handle, root-resolved: an alias is followed to the cell it
// was proven one with, and a handle holding no cell reads as a fresh one —
// the column's own absent contract.
fn row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, handle) -> RowCell with Memory + Alloc = match cell_at(spine, mint_bound, row_root(spine, mint_bound, handle, 0)) {
  CRow(cell) => cell,
  CRowAlias(_) | CEmpty => row_cell_fresh(0),
}

// Unconstrained: nothing charged here and no source but the cell itself. An
// instantiation's copy of a quantified leaf carries an edge from that leaf
// whose renaming names the copy — the subscription through which a group's
// still-growing declaration would reach it — and a source that IS this cell
// bounds nothing: the copy is a variable of its own until something else
// arrives.
//
// A cell bounded by a ceiling that admits nothing is not a variable either:
// its least solution is empty and nothing can ever be added without a
// refusal, so it is CLOSED — the form a row takes when the judgment proved
// no caller can reach it.
//
// The copy is a variable only when what it copies IS one. A source that
// holds content, reads other cells, or reads nothing because it was closed
// is a determined row, and its copy is that row renamed: reading the copy as
// free handed every call site a free root of its own for a callee row the
// callee had settled (measured 2026-09-24: `emit_expr` carried two, and its
// judgment rose 3.4MB → 94MB).
fn row_is_leaf(spine, mint_bound, self_root, cell: RowCell) =
  row_is_leaf_at(spine, mint_bound, self_root, cell, 0)

fn row_is_leaf_at(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, cell: RowCell, d) =
  len(cell.charged) == 0 && len(cell.full) == 0 && len(cell.frees) == 0 && all({ e => row_edge_is_self(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, d) }, cell.edges) && !any(row_ceiling_admits_nothing, cell.ceilings)

fn row_ceiling_admits_nothing(c: Ceiling) = match c.allowed {
  EfRow{present: p, tail: t} => match t {
    EtClosed => len(p) == 0,
    EtOpen(_) => false,
    EtAll => false,
  },
}

fn row_edge_is_self(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, e: RowEdge, d) = {
  let sr = row_root(spine, mint_bound, e.src, 0)
  if sr == self_root {
    true
  } else if e.inst && d < 32 {
    let to = row_tys_find(e.tys, sr)
    to >= 0 && row_root(spine, mint_bound, to, 0) == self_root && row_is_leaf_at(spine, mint_bound, sr, row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, sr), d + 1)
  } else {
    false
  }
}

// The projection every reader sees: the read, and as its tail the FREE roots
// it reaches. A bound root's content already arrived here across its own
// path's mask, and whatever it gains later is carried here the same way at
// the write, so a reader folding that content back in would re-derive it
// unmasked — the leak of an absorbed instance through a caller's tail. What
// a reader cannot know yet is only what a free root may still bring, so the
// tail names those, and the absent field is the mask every path to them
// shares: `present ∪ (tail ∖ absent)` never overstates what a root may still
// bring.
fn row_cell_projection(ref spine, mint_bound, handle, cell: RowCell) = if row_is_leaf(spine, mint_bound, row_root(spine, mint_bound, handle, 0), cell) {
  NRowFree(cell.born)
} else {
  let open = cell.frees |> filter({ (r, _) => row_root_is_free(spine, mint_bound, r) })
  if len(open) == 0 {
    NRowBound(EfRow(cell.full, [], EtClosed))
  } else {
    NRowBound(EfRow(cell.full, row_frees_common_mask(open), EtOpen(row_frees_roots(open, []))))
  }
}

fn row_root_is_free(ref spine, mint_bound, r) = {
  let root = row_root(spine, mint_bound, r, 0)
  row_is_leaf(spine, mint_bound, root, row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, root))
}

// The distinct roots, in first-reached order — several paths to one root
// are one tail entry.
fn row_frees_roots(frees, acc) = match frees {
  [] => acc,
  [f, ...rest] => match f {
    (r, _) => row_frees_roots(rest, if row_int_held(acc, r) { acc } else { push(acc, r) }),
  },
}

fn row_frees_common_mask(frees) = match frees {
  [] => [],
  [f, ...rest] => match f {
    (_, m) => row_masks_meet(m, rest),
  },
}

fn row_masks_meet(m, rest) = match rest {
  [] => m,
  [f, ...more] => match f {
    (_, m2) => row_masks_meet(row_names_common(m, m2), more),
  },
}

// Names two masks share, by effect identity — the absent field is a
// name-level summary; the instance a path handles lives on the path.
fn row_names_common(a, b) = match a {
  [] => [],
  [n, ...rest] => match row_mask_hit(b, n) {
    Some(_) => [n] ++ row_names_common(rest, b),
    None => row_names_common(rest, b),
  },
}

// A path joins the set unless the same path is already held. Two paths to
// one root stay TWO entries: each carries its own ordered mask, so an
// arrival meets the handler its own path installs first, and a bound
// travelling both ways lands twice — once per path — which is the MEET.
fn row_frees_add(frees, root, mask) =
  if row_frees_holds(frees, root, mask) { frees } else { push(frees, (root, mask)) }

// The paths a new free set holds that the old one did not — the roots a
// cell's bounds must now reach. The set only grows, and in order, so the
// new paths are its suffix.
fn row_frees_new(frees2, frees) =
  if len(frees2) == len(frees) { [] } else { slice(frees2, len(frees), len(frees2)) }

fn row_frees_holds(frees: [(Int, [EffName])], r, m) = if len(frees) == 0 {
  false
} else {
  match last(frees) {
    (r2, m2) => r2 == r && m2 == m || row_frees_holds(drop_last(frees), r, m),
  }
}

// Does this cell read `src_root` by any edge, and does it read anything
// across an instantiation?
fn row_edges_from(spine, mint_bound, edges: [RowEdge], src_root) =
  len(edges) > 0 && (row_root(spine, mint_bound, last(edges).src, 0) == src_root || row_edges_from(spine, mint_bound, drop_last(edges), src_root))

fn row_edges_inst(edges: [RowEdge]) =
  len(edges) > 0 && (last(edges).inst || row_edges_inst(drop_last(edges)))

// Does a new path reach a root some copy's renaming covers?
fn row_paths_keyed(spine, mint_bound, paths: [(Int, [EffName])], keyed) = if len(paths) == 0 {
  false
} else {
  match last(paths) {
    (r, _) => row_int_held(keyed, row_root(spine, mint_bound, r, 0)) || row_paths_keyed(spine, mint_bound, drop_last(paths), keyed),
  }
}

// ─── renaming a payload through an instantiation edge ────────────────────
fn row_tys_find(tys: [(Int, Int)], r) = match find({ (from, _) => from == r }, tys) {
  Some((_, to)) => to,
  None => -1,
}

// Only an instance's arguments rename, so a set holding none is its own
// renaming — and that is nearly every set an instantiation carries.
fn row_names_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, names, tys) = if len(tys) == 0 || !row_names_parameterized(names) {
  names
} else {
  names |> map({ n => row_name_rename(spine, mint_bound, n, tys) })
}

fn row_names_parameterized(names) =
  len(names) > 0 && (row_name_parameterized(last(names)) || row_names_parameterized(drop_last(names)))

fn row_name_parameterized(n) = match n {
  EParameterized{} => true,
  ENamed(_) => false,
  EPure => false,
}

fn row_name_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, n, tys) = match n {
  EParameterized{name: h, args} => EParameterized(h, row_args_rename(spine, mint_bound, args, tys)),
  ENamed(_) => n,
  EPure => n,
}

fn row_args_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, args, tys) =
  args |> map({ a => row_arg_rename(spine, mint_bound, a, tys) })

fn row_arg_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, a, tys) = match a {
  EAType(t) => EAType(ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, t, tys, 0)),
  EARow(h) => {
    let to = row_tys_find(tys, row_root(spine, mint_bound, h, 0))
    if to >= 0 { EARow(to) } else { a }
  },
  EAInt(_) => a,
  EAFloat(_) => a,
  EAString(_) => a,
  EANode(_) => a,
  EAEffect(_) => a,
  EACon(_) => a,
}

// A type under an instantiation's renaming: a quantified root becomes the
// fresh one this use owns; a bound variable is read through. Depth-guarded
// as every structural walk here is — the occurs guard keeps the graph acyclic.
fn ty_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, ty, tys, d) = if d > 200 {
  ty
} else {
  match ty {
    TInt => ty,
    TFloat => ty,
    TUnit => ty,
    TByte => ty,
    TVar(h) => {
      let r = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, h, 0)
      let to = row_tys_find(tys, r)
      if to >= 0 {
        TVar(to)
      } else {
        // An unrenamed free variable is its ROOT, so two copies of one class
        // are one value and a read holding both keeps one.
        match graph_node_at(spine, mint_bound, r) {
          GNode(NBound(inner), _) => ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, inner, tys, d + 1),
          _ => TVar(r),
        }
      }
    },
    TList(e) => TList(ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, e, tys, d + 1)),
    TTuple(es) => TTuple(ty_list_rename(spine, mint_bound, es, tys, d + 1)),
    TFun{params: ps, ret, row: eff} => TFun(
      ty_params_rename(spine, mint_bound, ps, tys, d + 1),
      ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, ret, tys, d + 1),
      row_value_rename(spine, mint_bound, eff, tys, d + 1)
    ),
    TName{name: n, args, rows} => TName(
      n,
      ty_list_rename(spine, mint_bound, args, tys, d + 1),
      row_roots_rename(spine, mint_bound, rows, tys)
    ),
    TRecord(fs) => TRecord(ty_fields_rename(spine, mint_bound, fs, tys, d + 1)),
    TRecordOpen{fields: fs, rest: v} => {
      let to = row_tys_find(tys, chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, v, 0))
      TRecordOpen(ty_fields_rename(spine, mint_bound, fs, tys, d + 1), if to >= 0 { to } else { v })
    },
    TRefined{base: b, pred: p} => TRefined(ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, b, tys, d + 1), p),
    TCont{input: i, answer: a, discipline: dsc, world: w} => TCont(
      ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, i, tys, d + 1),
      ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, a, tys, d + 1),
      dsc,
      row_value_rename(spine, mint_bound, w, tys, d + 1)
    ),
    TAlias{name: n, inner: i} => TAlias(n, ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, i, tys, d + 1)),
    TReprPin{repr: r, inner: b} => TReprPin(r, ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, b, tys, d + 1)),
  }
}

fn ty_list_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, tys_in, tys, d) = match tys_in {
  [] => [],
  [t, ...rest] => [ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, t, tys, d)] ++ ty_list_rename(spine, mint_bound, rest, tys, d),
}

fn ty_params_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, ps, tys, d) = match ps {
  [] => [],
  [p, ...rest] => [param_with_ty(p, ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, param_ty(p), tys, d))] ++ ty_params_rename(spine, mint_bound, rest, tys, d),
}

fn ty_fields_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, fs, tys, d) = match fs {
  [] => [],
  [f, ...rest] => match f {
    (n, t) => [(n, ty_rename(spine, mint_bound, t, tys, d))] ++ ty_fields_rename(spine, mint_bound, rest, tys, d),
  },
}

fn row_value_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, eff, tys, d) = match eff {
  EfRow{present: p, absent: a, tail: t} => EfRow(
    row_names_rename(spine, mint_bound, p, tys),
    row_names_rename(spine, mint_bound, a, tys),
    match t {
      EtOpen(vs) => EtOpen(row_roots_rename(spine, mint_bound, vs, tys)),
      EtClosed => t,
      EtAll => t,
    }
  ),
}

fn row_roots_rename(ref spine, mint_bound, vs, tys) = match vs {
  [] => [],
  [v, ...rest] => {
    let to = row_tys_find(tys, row_root(spine, mint_bound, v, 0))
    [if to >= 0 { to } else { v }] ++ row_roots_rename(spine, mint_bound, rest, tys)
  },
}

// ─── the mask — a handled INSTANCE, not a bare name ──────────────────────
// A name masked on an edge is filtered; when both it and the mask entry are
// instances whose arguments are not yet one, the crossing asks for their
// unification — the install's handled instance meets every instance that
// ever arrives across it, however late and through whatever parameter.
fn row_mask_filter(ref spine, mint_bound, names, mask, pairs) =
  if len(mask) == 0 { (names, pairs) } else { row_mask_walk(spine, mint_bound, names, mask, pairs) }

fn row_mask_walk(ref spine, mint_bound, names, mask, pairs) = fold(
  ([], pairs),
  (acc, n) => match acc {
    (kept, ps) => match row_mask_hit(mask, n) {
      None => (push(kept, n), ps),
      Some(m) => (kept, row_pair_note(spine, mint_bound, ps, m, n)),
    },
  },
  names
)

fn row_mask_hit(mask, n) = match mask {
  [] => None,
  [m, ...rest] => if eff_name_handle(m) == eff_name_handle(n) { Some(m) } else { row_mask_hit(rest, n) },
}

fn row_pair_note(ref spine, mint_bound, pairs, m, n) = match (m, n) {
  (EParameterized{args: ma}, EParameterized{args: na}) => if row_args_one(spine, mint_bound, ma, na) { pairs } else { push(pairs, (m, n)) },
  _ => pairs,
}

// Two instances' arguments already ONE — every type and row argument the same
// root. A charged instance carries its value dimensions first, a handler's
// mask never does, so they align on the unified arguments alone.
fn row_args_one(ref spine, mint_bound, ma, na) =
  zip(eff_unified_args(ma), eff_unified_args(na)) |> all({ pair => row_arg_same(spine, mint_bound, pair) })

// A row pair is met once one reads the other — the install includes a
// performer's row in the handler's, or the handler's in the performer's, by
// the direction the closure travels — so the pair is never asked for again.
fn row_arg_same(ref spine, mint_bound, pair) = match pair {
  (EAType(a), EAType(b)) => row_ty_same(spine, mint_bound, a, b),
  (EARow(x), EARow(y)) => {
    let rx = row_root(spine, mint_bound, x, 0)
    let ry = row_root(spine, mint_bound, y, 0)
    rx == ry || row_reads(spine, mint_bound, rx, ry) || row_reads(spine, mint_bound, ry, rx)
  },
  _ => false,
}

fn row_reads(ref spine, mint_bound, reader, src) = any(
  { e => !e.inst && row_root(spine, mint_bound, e.src, 0) == src },
  row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, reader).edges
)

// One type: the same root, or the same shape at the top once each side is
// read through its binding.
fn row_ty_same(ref spine, mint_bound, a, b) = match (row_ty_read(spine, mint_bound, a), row_ty_read(spine, mint_bound, b)) {
  (TVar(x), TVar(y)) => x == y,
  (TVar(_), _) => false,
  (_, TVar(_)) => false,
  (ta, tb) => ta == tb,
}

fn row_ty_read(ref spine, mint_bound, t) = match t {
  TVar(h) => {
    let r = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, h, 0)
    match graph_node_at(spine, mint_bound, r) {
      GNode(NBound(inner), _) => inner,
      _ => TVar(r),
    }
  },
  _ => t,
}

// ─── the read, recomputed from the sources ───────────────────────────────
// What was charged here ∪ each source's read, renamed and masked, then the
// cell's pins applied; the roots each source contributes (itself, and every
// root it reaches, each path's mask followed by this edge's). The fourth
// component is the SUBSCRIPTIONS the read now depends on beyond its own
// sources — an instantiation edge's renamed roots — for the carry to record.
fn row_recompute(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, cell: RowCell, pairs) = match fold(
  (cell.charged, cell.frees, pairs, []),
  (acc, e) => row_edge_into(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, acc),
  cell.edges
) {
  (full, frees, p2, subs) => (row_pin_names(full, cell.pins), frees, p2, subs),
}

// THE DELTA. A read is monotone, so what one source's change adds is read
// across the edges from that source alone and joined into the read already
// held — never a recompute from the charges. An instantiation edge is read
// again whole whatever woke it: the image it subscribed to is one of its
// renamed roots, not its source. Pins refine whatever arrives.
fn row_recompute_from(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, cell: RowCell, src_root, pairs) = match fold(
  (cell.full, cell.frees, pairs, []),
  (acc, e) => if e.inst || row_root(spine, mint_bound, e.src, 0) == src_root {
    row_edge_into(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, acc)
  } else {
    acc
  },
  cell.edges
) {
  (full, frees, p2, subs) => (if len(cell.pins) == 0 { full } else { row_pin_names(full, cell.pins) }, frees, p2, subs),
}

// ONE edge's delta — the edge just linked, read into what is already held.
fn row_recompute_edge(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, cell: RowCell, e: RowEdge, pairs) = match row_edge_into(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, (cell.full, cell.frees, pairs, [])) {
  (full, frees, p2, subs) => (if len(cell.pins) == 0 { full } else { row_pin_names(full, cell.pins) }, frees, p2, subs),
}

fn row_edge_into(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, e: RowEdge, acc) = match acc {
  (full, frees, pairs, subs) => {
    let sr = row_root(spine, mint_bound, e.src, 0)
    if sr == self_root {
      acc
    } else {
      let sc = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, sr)
      match row_mask_filter(
        spine,
        mint_bound,
        row_names_rename(spine, mint_bound, sc.full, e.tys),
        e.mask,
        pairs
      ) {
        (kept, p2) => if e.inst {
          row_inst_roots(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, sc.frees, (frag_union(full, kept), frees, p2, subs))
        } else {
          (frag_union(full, kept), row_frees_join(spine, mint_bound, sc.frees, e.mask, self_root, row_frees_add(frees, sr, e.mask)), p2, subs)
        },
      }
    }
  },
}

// AN INSTANTIATION EDGE CARRIES ITS SOURCE'S ROOTS, RENAMED, LIVE. The
// declaration's roots that the renaming covers are this use's own copies:
// each is read here — its content across the path's mask, its roots — and
// subscribed to, so a caller's closure arriving at a renamed parameter is
// charged to the renamed row, and a root the declaration gains after this
// copy was made is reached all the same. The roots the renaming does NOT
// cover are the declaration's private cells; their content already arrived
// through the source, and they are no root of this use.
fn row_inst_roots(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, e: RowEdge, src_frees, acc) = match src_frees {
  [] => acc,
  [f, ...rest] => match f {
    (r, m) => {
      let mr = row_tys_find(e.tys, row_root(spine, mint_bound, r, 0))
      let next = if mr < 0 {
        acc
      } else {
        let root = row_root(spine, mint_bound, mr, 0)
        if root == self_root {
          acc
        } else {
          match acc {
            (full, frees, pairs, subs) => {
              let path = row_mask_then(row_names_rename(spine, mint_bound, m, e.tys), e.mask)
              let mc = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, root)
              match row_mask_filter(spine, mint_bound, mc.full, path, pairs) {
                (kept, p2) => (frag_union(full, kept), row_frees_join(spine, mint_bound, mc.frees, path, self_root, row_frees_add(frees, root, path)), p2, if row_int_held(subs, root) { subs } else { push(subs, root) }),
              }
            },
          }
        }
      }
      row_inst_roots(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, rest, next)
    },
  },
}

// The source's paths, extended by this edge: its mask is OUTER to theirs.
// A path is held by its ROOT: a union after the path was recorded leaves the
// old handle an alias, and a path back to this cell is no root of it.
fn row_frees_join(ref spine, mint_bound, src_frees, mask, self_root, frees) = match src_frees {
  [] => frees,
  [f, ...rest] => match f {
    (r0, m) => {
      let r = row_root(spine, mint_bound, r0, 0)
      row_frees_join(
        spine,
        mint_bound,
        rest,
        mask,
        self_root,
        if r == self_root { frees } else { row_frees_add(frees, r, row_mask_then(m, mask)) }
      )
    },
  },
}

// A path's mask holds each effect ONCE, at the install that meets it first:
// an arrival stops at the first handler naming its effect, so an outer
// handler of the same effect is never reached along this path and has
// nothing to say. It is also what ends a masked cycle — the same install
// around the same recursion adds nothing the second time round.
fn row_mask_then(inner, outer) = if len(outer) == 0 {
  inner
} else if len(inner) == 0 {
  outer
} else {
  inner ++ row_names_without(outer, inner)
}

// ─── pins — a declaration's scalar instances ─────────────────────────────
// Charge 11: a declared `with Sample(48000)` refines every instance the
// cell reads whose value dimension its own charge could not know, from
// whichever source it arrived. Position-wise, scalars only — never a name
// added, never a type dimension (the install flow's).
fn row_pin_names(names, pins) = if len(pins) == 0 { names } else { row_pin_walk(names, pins, []) }

fn row_pin_walk(names, pins, acc) = match names {
  [] => acc,
  [n, ...rest] => row_pin_walk(rest, pins, frag_union(acc, [row_pin_one(n, pins)])),
}

fn row_pin_one(b, pins) = match b {
  EParameterized{name: bn, args: bargs} => match row_pin_source(pins, bn, bargs) {
    Some(dargs) => EParameterized(bn, row_pin_args(bargs, dargs, 0)),
    None => b,
  },
  ENamed(_) => b,
  EPure => b,
}

fn row_pin_source(pins, bn, bargs) = match pins {
  [] => None,
  [d, ...rest] => match d {
    EParameterized{name: dn, args: dargs} => if dn == bn && !eff_args_provably_distinct(dargs, bargs) {
      Some(dargs)
    } else {
      row_pin_source(rest, bn, bargs)
    },
    ENamed(_) => row_pin_source(rest, bn, bargs),
    EPure => row_pin_source(rest, bn, bargs),
  },
}

fn row_pin_args(bargs, dargs, i) = if i >= len(bargs) {
  []
} else if i >= len(dargs) {
  [list_index(bargs, i)] ++ row_pin_args(bargs, dargs, i + 1)
} else {
  [row_pin_arg(list_index(bargs, i), list_index(dargs, i))] ++ row_pin_args(bargs, dargs, i + 1)
}

// A charge-side value dim (EANode) takes the declared constant; a type dim
// is the install flow's, never value-pinned; a concrete scalar keeps its
// own truth, which the bounds judge.
fn row_pin_arg(barg, darg) = match barg {
  EANode(_) => match darg {
    EAType(_) => barg,
    EAInt(_) => darg,
    EAFloat(_) => darg,
    EAString(_) => darg,
    EAEffect(_) => darg,
    EACon(_) => darg,
    EANode(_) => darg,
    EARow(_) => barg,
  },
  EAType(_) => barg,
  EARow(_) => barg,
  EAInt(_) => barg,
  EAFloat(_) => barg,
  EAString(_) => barg,
  EAEffect(_) => barg,
  EACon(_) => barg,
}

// Entries of `a` absent from `b` by fragment identity — what a read gained.
fn row_frag_minus(a, b) = if len(a) == 0 {
  []
} else {
  let kept = row_frag_minus(drop_last(a), b)
  let x = last(a)
  if frag_holds(b, x) { kept } else { push(kept, x) }
}

// ─── the carry — a worklist over the reverse edges ───────────────────────
// A wake names what woke it: the source whose read grew (a dependent reads
// across the edges from that source alone and joins in what they add), or
// ALL — a cell whose own terms changed shape (a new pin refines what already
// arrived) and must be read again from its charges.
// A growth carries WHAT grew — the names and the paths the source gained —
// so a reader joins that and never walks the source's whole read again: a
// frame cell a large function links every call into gains a path per call,
// and re-reading all of them at each of its readers made one judgment
// quadratic in its calls (measured 2026-09-24: `emit_expr`'s cell held 458
// edges, 483 paths and 42 readers, and its judgment wrote 89MB of rows). A
// union has no delta — the class's read is new to both sides — so it wakes
// with a whole re-read of the source.
type RowWake
  = WakeDelta(reader: Int, src: Int, names: [EffName], paths: [(Int, [EffName])])
  | WakeFrom(Int, Int)
  | WakeEdge(Int, RowEdge)
  | WakeAll(Int)

// A row cell's write. The inverse is recorded only for an open speculation —
// the one reader an undo entry has.
fn row_put(ref spine, mint_bound, h, slot, acc: RowAcc) = if acc.undo {
  let old = cell_slot_at(spine, h)
  spine_put_cell(spine, h, slot)
  ({...acc, tl: acc.tl + 1, trail: trail_append(acc.trail, acc.tl, MSetRow(h, old)), writes: acc.writes + 1})
} else {
  spine_put_cell(spine, h, slot)
  ({...acc, writes: acc.writes + 1})
}

// A read that reaches a root through an instantiation's renaming depends on
// it: the root's reverse edge learns the reader, trailed like every write.
fn row_subscribe(ref spine, mint_bound, reader, subs, acc: RowAcc) = fold(
  acc,
  (a, s) => {
    let rs = row_root(spine, mint_bound, s, 0)
    if !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, rs) {
      a
    } else {
      let cs = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, rs)
      if row_int_held(cs.deps, reader) {
        a
      } else {
        row_put(spine, mint_bound, rs, CRow({...cs, deps: push(cs.deps, reader)}), a)
      }
    }
  },
  subs
)

// An instantiation joins its source's copies, and the roots its renaming
// covers join the source's keys. The copy is new, so it joins without the
// membership read; a key is read for, because every use of one declaration
// renames the same roots.
fn row_depend(ref spine, mint_bound, src, reader, tys: [(Int, Int)], acc: RowAcc) = if !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, src) {
  acc
} else {
  let cs = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, src)
  row_put(
    spine,
    mint_bound,
    src,
    CRow({...cs, copies: push(cs.copies, reader), keyed: row_keys_join(spine, mint_bound, cs.keyed, tys)}),
    acc
  )
}

// A renaming also covers the declaration's TYPE variables, and a type
// variable is never a path's root, so only the row cells it renames are keys:
// `map`'s four keys were two rows and two dead entries.
fn row_keys_join(spine, mint_bound, keyed, tys: [(Int, Int)]) = if len(tys) == 0 {
  keyed
} else {
  match last(tys) {
    (k, _) => row_keys_join(
      spine,
      mint_bound,
      if row_int_held(keyed, k) || !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, row_root(spine, mint_bound, k, 0)) {
        keyed
      } else {
        push(keyed, k)
      },
      drop_last(tys)
    ),
  }
}

// Every reader of `src`, direct or a copy, is woken by it, to read it again
// whole.
fn row_wake_deps(work, cell: RowCell, src) =
  fold(work, (w, d) => push(w, WakeFrom(d, src)), cell.deps ++ cell.copies)

// A cell's read grew: write it, meet its bounds with what is new — the names
// against every bound, the new roots taking every bound onto themselves — and
// hand whoever reads it exactly what is new. A copy reads a new path only
// through its renaming, so a growth that brings no name and reaches no
// renamed root is handed to the direct readers alone. A frame a large
// function links every call into gains a path per call, and its in-group
// copies learned nothing from any of them (measured 2026-09-24: `emit_expr`'s
// 458 links each consulted 42 copies, 5.7MB of its judgment).
fn row_grown(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, root, cell: RowCell, full2, frees2, work, acc: RowAcc) = {
  let cell2 = {...cell, frees: frees2, full: full2}
  let new_names = row_frag_minus(full2, cell.full)
  let new_paths = row_frees_new(frees2, cell.frees)
  let acc2 = row_put(spine, mint_bound, root, CRow(cell2), acc)
  let acc3 = row_check_names(spine, spine_open, cell2.ceilings, new_names, acc2)
  let acc4 = row_ceilings_onto(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, cell2.ceilings, new_paths, acc3)
  let copies_learn = len(new_names) > 0 || row_paths_keyed(spine, mint_bound, new_paths, cell2.keyed)
  let readers = if copies_learn { cell2.deps ++ cell2.copies } else { cell2.deps }
  (fold(work, (w, d) => push(w, WakeDelta(d, root, new_names, new_paths)), readers), acc4)
}

// A source's growth, joined across what connects this cell to it: each edge
// from it — the names renamed and masked by that edge, the new paths extended
// by it, an instantiation's new roots read as this use's copies — and each
// path this cell subscribed to at it, masked by that path. Nothing the source
// held before is read again.
fn row_recompute_delta(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, cell: RowCell, src_root, names, paths, pairs) = {
  // A subscription is a path an instantiation edge's renaming reached, so
  // only a cell reading across an instantiation holds one; a cell that reads
  // this source only by edges has nothing more to look for among its paths.
  let direct = row_edges_from(spine, mint_bound, cell.edges, src_root)
  let by_edges = if direct {
    fold(
      (cell.full, cell.frees, pairs, []),
      (acc, e) => if row_root(spine, mint_bound, e.src, 0) == src_root {
        row_edge_delta(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, names, paths, acc)
      } else {
        acc
      },
      cell.edges
    )
  } else {
    (cell.full, cell.frees, pairs, [])
  }
  let joined = if !direct || row_edges_inst(cell.edges) {
    fold(
      by_edges,
      (acc, f) => match f {
        (r, m) => if row_root(spine, mint_bound, r, 0) == src_root {
          row_path_delta(spine, mint_bound, self_root, m, names, paths, acc)
        } else {
          acc
        },
      },
      cell.frees
    )
  } else {
    by_edges
  }
  match joined {
    (full, frees, p2, subs) => (if len(cell.pins) == 0 { full } else { row_pin_names(full, cell.pins) }, frees, p2, subs),
  }
}

// A delta with no names adds none, so its names are read only when there are
// some: most wakes of a large cell's readers carry one new path and nothing
// else, and the rename, the mask and the union were paid to learn that.
fn row_edge_delta(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, e: RowEdge, names, paths, acc) = {
  let named = if len(names) == 0 {
    acc
  } else {
    row_names_delta(spine, mint_bound, row_names_rename(spine, mint_bound, names, e.tys), e.mask, acc)
  }
  if e.inst {
    row_inst_roots(spine, mint_bound, self_root, e, paths, named)
  } else if len(paths) == 0 {
    named
  } else {
    match named {
      (full, frees, pairs, subs) => (full, row_frees_join(spine, mint_bound, paths, e.mask, self_root, frees), pairs, subs),
    }
  }
}

fn row_path_delta(ref spine, mint_bound, self_root, m, names, paths, acc) = {
  let named = if len(names) == 0 { acc } else { row_names_delta(spine, mint_bound, names, m, acc) }
  if len(paths) == 0 {
    named
  } else {
    match named {
      (full, frees, pairs, subs) => (full, row_frees_join(spine, mint_bound, paths, m, self_root, frees), pairs, subs),
    }
  }
}

fn row_names_delta(ref spine, mint_bound, names, mask, acc) = match acc {
  (full, frees, pairs, subs) => match row_mask_filter(spine, mint_bound, names, mask, pairs) {
    (kept, p2) => (frag_union(full, kept), frees, p2, subs),
  },
}

fn row_carry(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, work, acc: RowAcc, fuel) = if len(work) == 0 {
  acc
} else if fuel <= 0 {
  eprint_string("mentl: a row carry exhausted its belt — a monotone propagation did not settle\n")
  acc
} else {
  let wake = last(work)
  let m0 = heap_mark()
  match row_carry_one(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, drop_last(work), wake, acc) {
    (work2, acc2) => {
      row_tally_add(acc2.tally, row_tally_of_wake(wake), heap_mark() - m0)
      row_carry(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, work2, acc2, fuel - 1)
    },
  }
}

// One wake: its target read again across what woke it, and — when the read
// grew — written and handed on to whoever reads the target.
fn row_carry_one(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, rest, wake, acc: RowAcc) = {
  let target = match wake {
    WakeDelta{reader: t} => t,
    WakeFrom(t, _) => t,
    WakeEdge(t, _) => t,
    WakeAll(t) => t,
  }
  let root = row_root(spine, mint_bound, target, 0)
  if !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, root) {
    (rest, acc)
  } else {
    let cell = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, root)
    let read = match wake {
      WakeAll(_) => row_recompute(spine, mint_bound, root, cell, acc.pairs),
      WakeDelta{src: s, names, paths} => row_recompute_delta(
        spine,
        mint_bound,
        root,
        cell,
        row_root(spine, mint_bound, s, 0),
        names,
        paths,
        acc.pairs
      ),
      WakeFrom(_, s) => row_recompute_from(spine, mint_bound, root, cell, row_root(spine, mint_bound, s, 0), acc.pairs),
      WakeEdge(_, e) => row_recompute_edge(spine, mint_bound, root, cell, e, acc.pairs),
    }
    match read {
      (full2, frees2, pairs2, subs) => {
        let acc_p = row_subscribe(spine, mint_bound, root, subs, row_acc_with_pairs(acc, pairs2))
        let grew = match wake {
          WakeAll(_) => true,
          WakeDelta{} => len(full2) != len(cell.full) || len(frees2) != len(cell.frees),
          WakeFrom(_, _) => len(full2) != len(cell.full) || len(frees2) != len(cell.frees),
          WakeEdge(_, _) => len(full2) != len(cell.full) || len(frees2) != len(cell.frees),
        }
        if grew {
          row_grown(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, root, cell, full2, frees2, rest, acc_p)
        } else {
          (rest, acc_p)
        }
      },
    }
  }
}

// ─── the writes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A charge adds to the cell's own terms and, pinned, to its read — no source
// is read again, since none changed.
fn row_teach_names(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, handle, names, acc: RowAcc) = {
  let root = row_root(spine, mint_bound, handle, 0)
  if !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, root) {
    acc
  } else {
    let cell = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, root)
    let own2 = frag_union(cell.charged, names)
    if len(own2) == len(cell.charged) {
      acc
    } else {
      let fresh = row_frag_minus(names, cell.charged)
      let cell1 = {...cell, charged: own2}
      let full2 = frag_union(cell.full, row_pin_names(fresh, cell.pins))
      if len(full2) == len(cell.full) {
        row_put(spine, mint_bound, root, CRow(cell1), acc)
      } else {
        match row_grown(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, root, cell1, full2, cell.frees, [], acc) {
          (work, acc2) => row_carry(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, work, acc2, row_carry_fuel),
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn row_edge_same(a: RowEdge, b: RowEdge) =
  a.src == b.src && a.inst == b.inst && a.mask == b.mask && a.tys == b.tys

fn row_edge_held(edges: [RowEdge], e) =
  len(edges) > 0 && (row_edge_same(last(edges), e) || row_edge_held(drop_last(edges), e))

fn row_link_cells(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, into, e0: RowEdge, acc: RowAcc) = {
  let ri = row_root(spine, mint_bound, into, 0)
  let rs = row_root(spine, mint_bound, e0.src, 0)
  if ri == rs || !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, ri) {
    acc
  } else {
    let ci = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, ri)
    let e = RowEdge{inst: e0.inst, mask: e0.mask, src: rs, tys: e0.tys}
    // An INSTANTIATION edge is made once, into the copy minted for this use,
    // so nothing can hold it yet and the copy is no dependent yet: neither
    // question is asked. Asking cost one scan of the declaration's dependents
    // per use — a list one entry longer at every call site in the program.
    // Any other link subscribes its reader to its source, so an edge from
    // this source can be held only if the reader already is one of the
    // source's dependents; that short list answers first, and the reader's
    // own edges are read only then.
    if !e.inst && row_int_held(row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, rs).deps, ri) && row_edge_held(ci.edges, e) {
      acc
    } else {
      let acc2 = row_put(spine, mint_bound, ri, CRow({...ci, edges: push(ci.edges, e)}), acc)
      let acc3 = if e.inst {
        row_depend(spine, mint_bound, rs, ri, e.tys, acc2)
      } else {
        row_subscribe(spine, mint_bound, ri, [rs], acc2)
      }
      // The new edge is the whole delta: read it alone.
      row_carry(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, [WakeEdge(ri, e)], acc3, row_carry_fuel)
    }
  }
}

// The merged class reads each outside source once, held by its ROOT: an edge
// recorded before an earlier union names an alias, and an edge from either
// side of this union is the class reading itself — a self-loop that would
// make a free cell look bound.
fn row_edges_merge(ref spine, mint_bound, a, b: [RowEdge], ra, rb) = match b {
  [] => a,
  [e0, ...rest] => {
    let rs = row_root(spine, mint_bound, e0.src, 0)
    let e = RowEdge{inst: e0.inst, mask: e0.mask, src: rs, tys: e0.tys}
    row_edges_merge(
      spine,
      mint_bound,
      if rs == ra || rs == rb || row_edge_held(a, e) { a } else { push(a, e) },
      rest,
      ra,
      rb
    )
  },
}

fn row_ints_merge(a, b) = match b {
  [] => a,
  [x, ...rest] => row_ints_merge(if row_int_held(a, x) { a } else { push(a, x) }, rest),
}

fn row_frees_merge(ref spine, mint_bound, a, b, ra, rb) = match b {
  [] => a,
  [f, ...rest] => match f {
    (r0, m) => {
      let r = row_root(spine, mint_bound, r0, 0)
      row_frees_merge(
        spine,
        mint_bound,
        if r == ra || r == rb { a } else { row_frees_add(a, r, m) },
        rest,
        ra,
        rb
      )
    },
  },
}

fn row_ceilings_merge(a, b) = match b {
  [] => a,
  [c, ...rest] => row_ceilings_merge(if row_ceiling_held(a, c) { a } else { push(a, c) }, rest),
}

fn row_union_cells(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, a, b, acc: RowAcc) = {
  let ra = row_root(spine, mint_bound, a, 0)
  let rb = row_root(spine, mint_bound, b, 0)
  if ra == rb || !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, ra) || !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, rb) {
    acc
  } else {
    let ca = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, ra)
    let cb = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, rb)
    let merged = RowCell{born: ca.born, ceilings: row_ceilings_merge(ca.ceilings, cb.ceilings), charged: frag_union(ca.charged, cb.charged), copies: row_ints_merge(ca.copies, cb.copies), deps: row_ints_merge(ca.deps, cb.deps), edges: row_edges_merge(
      spine,
      mint_bound,
      row_edges_merge(spine, mint_bound, [], ca.edges, ra, rb),
      cb.edges,
      ra,
      rb
    ), frees: row_frees_merge(
      spine,
      mint_bound,
      row_frees_merge(spine, mint_bound, [], ca.frees, ra, rb),
      cb.frees,
      ra,
      rb
    ), full: frag_union(ca.full, cb.full), keyed: row_ints_merge(ca.keyed, cb.keyed), pins: frag_union(ca.pins, cb.pins)}
    let acc2 = row_put(spine, mint_bound, ra, CRow(merged), acc)
    let acc3 = row_put(spine, mint_bound, rb, CRowAlias(ra), acc2)
    // Each side's bounds now meet the other side's read and roots.
    let acc4 = row_check_names(spine, spine_open, ca.ceilings, row_frag_minus(cb.full, ca.full), acc3)
    let acc5 = row_check_names(spine, spine_open, cb.ceilings, row_frag_minus(ca.full, cb.full), acc4)
    let acc6 = row_ceilings_onto(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, ca.ceilings, cb.frees, acc5)
    let acc7 = row_ceilings_onto(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, cb.ceilings, ca.frees, acc6)
    // A pin on either side refines the other's arrivals: read the merged
    // cell again whole, then everyone who read either side.
    row_carry(
      spine,
      spine_open,
      mint_bound,
      row_wake_deps([WakeAll(ra)], merged, ra),
      acc7,
      row_carry_fuel
    )
  }
}

// ─── ceilings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Does a bound admit one name? A closed bound admits by the instance law's
// positive side; a negation admits whatever it does not forbid.
fn row_ceiling_admits(allowed, n) = match allowed {
  EfRow{present: p, absent: a, tail: t} => match t {
    EtAll => !row_names_forbid(a, n),
    EtClosed => row_names_admit(p, n),
    EtOpen(_) => row_names_admit(p, n),
  },
}

fn row_names_forbid(forbidden, n) = match forbidden {
  [] => false,
  [f, ...rest] => if eff_forbids(f, n) { true } else { row_names_forbid(rest, n) },
}

fn row_names_admit(gate, n) = match gate {
  [] => false,
  [g, ...rest] => if eff_admits(g, n) { true } else { row_names_admit(rest, n) },
}

// allowed(inner) ⊆ allowed(outer): a sealed bound meeting a crossing one.
fn row_allowed_within(inner, outer) = match inner {
  EfRow{present: ip, absent: ia, tail: it} => match it {
    EtAll => match outer {
      EfRow{absent: oa, tail: ot} => match ot {
        EtAll => row_all_forbidden(oa, ia),
        EtClosed => false,
        EtOpen(_) => false,
      },
    },
    EtClosed => row_all_admitted(ip, outer),
    EtOpen(_) => row_all_admitted(ip, outer),
  },
}

fn row_all_admitted(names, outer) = match names {
  [] => true,
  [n, ...rest] => if row_ceiling_admits(outer, n) { row_all_admitted(rest, outer) } else { false },
}

// Every name `outer_forbidden` holds, `inner_forbidden` holds too.
fn row_all_forbidden(outer_forbidden, inner_forbidden) = match outer_forbidden {
  [] => true,
  [f, ...rest] => if row_names_forbid(inner_forbidden, f) { row_all_forbidden(rest, inner_forbidden) } else { false },
}

// A bound widened by a path's mask: the masked names cannot reach the root
// across that path, so the root may carry them.
fn row_ceiling_widen(c: Ceiling, mask) = if len(mask) == 0 {
  Ceiling{allowed: c.allowed, at: c.at, sealed: false, why: c.why}
} else {
  match c.allowed {
    EfRow{present: p, absent: a, tail: t} => match t {
      EtAll => Ceiling{allowed: EfRow([], row_names_without(a, mask), EtAll), at: c.at, sealed: false, why: c.why},
      _ => Ceiling{allowed: EfRow(frag_union(p, mask), [], EtClosed), at: c.at, sealed: false, why: c.why},
    },
  }
}

fn row_names_without(names, mask) = match names {
  [] => [],
  [n, ...rest] => match row_mask_hit(mask, n) {
    Some(_) => row_names_without(rest, mask),
    None => [n] ++ row_names_without(rest, mask),
  },
}

fn row_ceiling_held(ceilings: [Ceiling], c: Ceiling) = match ceilings {
  [] => false,
  [x, ...rest] => if x.at == c.at && x.allowed == c.allowed { true } else { row_ceiling_held(rest, c) },
}

fn row_sealed_of(ceilings: [Ceiling]) = match ceilings {
  [] => None,
  [c, ...rest] => if c.sealed { Some(c) } else { row_sealed_of(rest) },
}

// The module a node was born in, read off its own column — the arm's
// coordinates-only discipline (no op, no intern read).
fn spine_module_of(ref spine, spine_open, h) with Memory + Alloc + Cast = if h < 0 || spine_band(h) >= spine_open {
  0
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, h).module, spine_slot(h))
  if cell == 0 { 0 } else { cell - 1 }
}

// A refusal the arm cannot raise: the bound, the name that broke it, AT the
// node that declared the bound. The line is left for the body to render —
// rendering a name reads the intern table, which an arm must not perform.
// An absolute Pure is its own refusal — the body was declared to do nothing.
fn row_violation(ref spine, spine_open, c: Ceiling, got) with Memory + Alloc + Cast = {
  let span = spine_span_at(spine, spine_open, c.at)
  let d = match c.allowed {
    EfRow{present: [], absent: [], tail: EtClosed} => EPurityViolated(got, span),
    _ => EEffectMismatch(c.allowed, got, span),
  }
  (d, "", spine_module_name(spine, spine_open, spine_module_of(spine, spine_open, c.at)))
}

fn row_check_names(ref spine, spine_open, ceilings, names, acc: RowAcc) = if len(names) == 0 {
  acc
} else {
  match ceilings {
    [] => acc,
    [c, ...rest] => row_check_names(spine, spine_open, rest, names, row_check_one(spine, spine_open, c, names, acc)),
  }
}

fn row_check_one(ref spine, spine_open, c: Ceiling, names, acc: RowAcc) = match names {
  [] => acc,
  [n, ...rest] => row_check_one(
    spine,
    spine_open,
    c,
    rest,
    if row_ceiling_admits(c.allowed, n) {
      acc
    } else {
      row_acc_with_diag(acc, row_violation(spine, spine_open, c, EfRow([n], [], EtClosed)))
    }
  ),
}

fn row_ceilings_onto(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, ceilings, frees, acc: RowAcc) = if len(frees) == 0 {
  acc
} else {
  match ceilings {
    [] => acc,
    [c, ...rest] => row_ceilings_onto(
      spine,
      spine_open,
      mint_bound,
      rest,
      frees,
      row_ceiling_onto_frees(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, c, frees, acc, 0)
    ),
  }
}

fn row_ceiling_onto_frees(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, c: Ceiling, frees, acc: RowAcc, depth) = match frees {
  [] => acc,
  [f, ...rest] => match f {
    (r, m) => row_ceiling_onto_frees(
      spine,
      spine_open,
      mint_bound,
      c,
      rest,
      row_ceiling_install(
        spine,
        spine_open,
        mint_bound,
        row_root(spine, mint_bound, r, 0),
        row_ceiling_widen(c, m),
        acc,
        depth
      ),
      depth
    ),
  },
}

// INSTALL a bound on a root. A root already holding it stops the walk; a
// root carrying the author's own SEALED bound is checked, never
// strengthened; otherwise the bound joins, meets the root's read, and
// travels on to the root's own free roots.
fn row_ceiling_install(ref spine, spine_open, mint_bound, root, c: Ceiling, acc: RowAcc, depth) = if depth > 64 {
  acc
} else if !cell_slot_held(spine, mint_bound, root) {
  acc
} else {
  let cell = row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, root)
  if row_ceiling_held(cell.ceilings, c) {
    acc
  } else {
    match if c.sealed { None } else { row_sealed_of(cell.ceilings) } {
      Some(s) => if row_allowed_within(s.allowed, c.allowed) {
        acc
      } else {
        row_acc_with_diag(acc, row_violation(spine, spine_open, c, s.allowed))
      },
      None => {
        let acc2 = row_put(spine, mint_bound, root, CRow({...cell, ceilings: push(cell.ceilings, c)}), acc)
        let acc3 = row_check_one(spine, spine_open, c, cell.full, acc2)
        row_ceiling_onto_frees(spine, spine_open, mint_bound, c, cell.frees, acc3, depth + 1)
      },
    }
  }
}

// The one nodes read. Every minted handle's cell was written at its own
// mint, so below the bound only PLAN GAPS are unwritten (a planned range's
// final mints running under its trial measure — the cell stays virgin);
// the word-face guard answers those and any never-minted defensive read
// with the synthesized NFree, and a live handle reads its cell directly.
fn graph_node_at(ref spine, mint_bound, idx) with Memory + Alloc + Cast = if idx >= mint_bound {
  GNode(NFree(0), Fresh(idx))
} else {
  let cell = list_index(spine_page(spine, idx).nodes, spine_slot(idx))
  if addr(cell) == 0 { GNode(NFree(0), Fresh(idx)) } else { cell }
}

// Occurs check
// Before graph_bind(h, ty, r): walk ty for free handles containing h.
// If found, inference emits E_OccursCheck and refuses. No silent
// binding. No cycle except through explicit <~ feedback.
//
// Iteration uses flat-indexed loops on list_to_flat'd inputs — snoc
// inputs materialize once at entry, flat inputs pass through. Walks
// short-circuit on first hit.
//
// H6: exhaustive Ty enumeration. A wildcard arm here silently
// misses a new Ty variant's containment check — the most
// dangerous site in the compiler because a missed occurs-check
// admits infinite types. Every current Ty variant is named; any
// future variant fails to compile until its containment semantics
// are decided explicitly.
//
// THE WALK IS A REACHABILITY QUERY OVER A DAG, AND ITS VISITED SET IS A
// COLUMN (2026-09-21, re-founded 2026-09-24). Var roots are shared: a cycle's
// every member binds a TFun whose row edges reach the same hub vars, so a
// walk that descends each edge afresh visits a hub once PER PATH — one bind
// of a two-line render fn's call allocated 170MB that way, 40% of the whole
// judgment's heap. The first visited set was a TABLE minted per check
// (`wmap_new(256)`, 1,032 bytes at the first root) with a `(Bool, seen)`
// pair returned from every arm, and `mentl verify`'s tally filed the check
// at 226MB of the wheel's 819MB judgment — 28%, the largest class it names.
// A root's mark is a cell in the spine's `mark` column now, written with the
// check's STAMP — the epoch its bind takes — so a root explored once is one
// word to ask about, and the check allocates nothing at all.
fn occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ty, stamp) with !Mutate = match ty {
  TInt => false,
  TFloat => false,
  TUnit => false,
  TByte => false,
  TVar(id) => // The leaf answers "does handle occur in the type GRAPH reachable from
  // here" — three tiers, each a hole the selfapply fixture found trapping
  // the compiler (f(f) spun chase_deep/subst_ty to stack exhaustion with
  // ZERO occurs reports): (1) the raw handle; (2) the ROOT (unify IS
  // union-find — the indirect cycle a ~ (b -> r) with b's root already a
  // passed a raw compare); (3) the root's BINDING, recursed — bind
  // graph_bind(a, TFun([b], r)) first (b free, passes), then close the
  // cycle with graph_bind(b, TVar(a)):
  // no handle comparison sees that cycle, only descending a's bound
  // structure does. Termination is the guarded-write invariant itself:
  // every bind runs this check, so the graph stays acyclic and the
  // recursion always bottoms out (chase_deep's depth tripwire is the
  // belt behind this suspender).
  id == handle || {
    let root = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, id, 0)
    root == handle || !marked(spine, mint_bound, root, stamp) && {
      mark(spine, mint_bound, root, stamp)
      match chase_node(spine, mint_bound, root, 0) {
        GNode(NBound(inner), _) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, inner, stamp),
        GNode(NRecordRowBound{fields}, _) => occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields, stamp),
        _ => false,
      }
    }
  },
  TList(elem) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, elem, stamp),
  TTuple(elems) => occurs_in_list(spine, mint_bound, handle, elems, stamp),
  TFun{params, ret, row: eff} => occurs_in_params(spine, mint_bound, handle, params, stamp) || occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ret, stamp) || occurs_in_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, eff, stamp),
  TName{args, rows} => occurs_in_list(spine, mint_bound, handle, args, stamp) || occurs_in_edges(spine, mint_bound, handle, rows, stamp),
  TRecord(fields) => occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields, stamp),
  TRecordOpen{fields, rest: v} => occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields, stamp) || occurs_in_record_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, v, stamp),
  TRefined{base} => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, base, stamp),
  TCont{input, answer, world} => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, input, stamp) || occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, answer, stamp) || occurs_in_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, world, stamp),
  TAlias{inner} => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, inner, stamp),
  TReprPin{inner: base} => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, base, stamp),
}

// The grade write's contract, read pairwise from the end: the graded list is
// the held list with only each param's `resolved` slot moved. Name, authored
// marker and default must be the same value; the TYPE must be the same type
// as the graph holds it — the held fn type and the graded one were built one
// unification apart (prereg's param cells and the body's are aliases), so a
// value compare would refuse every pre-registered fn.
fn grades_only_differ(spine, mint_bound, was, params) with !Mutate = len(was) == len(params) && (len(was) == 0 || {
  let w = last(was)
  let p = last(params)
  param_name(w) == param_name(p) && param_authored(w) == param_authored(p) && param_default(w) == param_default(p) && ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, param_ty(w), param_ty(p)) && grades_only_differ(spine, mint_bound, drop_last(was), drop_last(params))
})

// A type read at its root: a variable chased to the cell it stands for, an
// alias to what it names. The structure compare below reads both sides here
// first, so two spellings of one proof compare as the one proof.
fn ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, t) with !Mutate = match t {
  TVar(h) => {
    // The root is the type wherever it holds no structure — free, a presence
    // cell, a hole: the variable itself is what the graph knows there.
    let root = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, h, 0)
    let as_itself = TVar(root)
    match chase_node(spine, mint_bound, root, 0) {
      GNode(NBound(inner), _) => match inner {
        TVar(_) => as_itself,
        _ => ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, inner),
      },
      _ => as_itself,
    }
  },
  TAlias{inner} => ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, inner),
  _ => t,
}

// Two types the graph already holds equal: the same structure once every
// variable is read at its root, so a unification of them would bind nothing.
fn ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, a, b) with !Mutate = match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, a) {
  TVar(ra) => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TVar(rb) => ra == rb,
    _ => false,
  },
  TInt => ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) == TInt,
  TFloat => ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) == TFloat,
  TUnit => ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) == TUnit,
  TByte => ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) == TByte,
  TList(ea) => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TList(eb) => ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, ea, eb),
    _ => false,
  },
  TTuple(xs) => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TTuple(ys) => tys_agree(spine, mint_bound, xs, ys),
    _ => false,
  },
  TFun{params: pa, ret: ra, row: ea} => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TFun{params: pb, ret: rb, row: eb} => params_agree(spine, mint_bound, pa, pb) && ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, ra, rb) && rows_agree(spine, mint_bound, ea, eb),
    _ => false,
  },
  TName{name: na, args: xa, rows: ra} => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TName{name: nb, args: xb, rows: rb} => na == nb && tys_agree(spine, mint_bound, xa, xb) && row_roots_agree(spine, mint_bound, ra, rb),
    _ => false,
  },
  TRecord(fa) => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TRecord(fb) => fields_agree(spine, mint_bound, fa, fb),
    _ => false,
  },
  TRecordOpen{fields: fa, rest: va} => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TRecordOpen{fields: fb, rest: vb} => fields_agree(spine, mint_bound, fa, fb) && chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, va, 0) == chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, vb, 0),
    _ => false,
  },
  TRefined{base: ba, pred: pa} => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TRefined{base: bb, pred: pb} => ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, ba, bb) && pa == pb,
    _ => false,
  },
  TReprPin{repr: ra, inner: ia} => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TReprPin{repr: rb, inner: ib} => ra == rb && ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, ia, ib),
    _ => false,
  },
  TCont{input: ia, answer: aa, discipline: da, world: wa} => match ty_resolve(spine, mint_bound, b) {
    TCont{input: ib, answer: ab, discipline: db, world: wb} => ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, ia, ib) && ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, aa, ab) && da == db && rows_agree(spine, mint_bound, wa, wb),
    _ => false,
  },
  TAlias{inner} => ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, inner, b),
}

fn tys_agree(spine, mint_bound, xs, ys) with !Mutate =
  len(xs) == len(ys) && (len(xs) == 0 || ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, last(xs), last(ys)) && tys_agree(spine, mint_bound, drop_last(xs), drop_last(ys)))

fn params_agree(spine, mint_bound, pa, pb) with !Mutate =
  len(pa) == len(pb) && (len(pa) == 0 || param_name(last(pa)) == param_name(last(pb)) && ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, param_ty(last(pa)), param_ty(last(pb))) && params_agree(spine, mint_bound, drop_last(pa), drop_last(pb)))

fn fields_agree(spine, mint_bound, fa, fb) with !Mutate = len(fa) == len(fb) && (len(fa) == 0 || match last(fa) {
  (na, ta) => match last(fb) {
    (nb, tb) => na == nb && ty_agrees(spine, mint_bound, ta, tb) && fields_agree(spine, mint_bound, drop_last(fa), drop_last(fb)),
  },
})

// Two rows agree when their names agree as values and their tails reach the
// same cells: an open tail is a set of row roots, read here as one.
fn rows_agree(spine, mint_bound, ea, eb) with !Mutate = match ea {
  EfRow{present: pa, absent: aa, tail: ta} => match eb {
    EfRow{present: pb, absent: ab, tail: tb} => pa == pb && aa == ab && match ta {
      EtOpen(vs) => match tb {
        EtOpen(ws) => row_roots_agree(spine, mint_bound, vs, ws),
        _ => false,
      },
      EtClosed => tb == EtClosed,
      EtAll => tb == EtAll,
    },
  },
}

fn row_roots_agree(spine, mint_bound, vs, ws) with !Mutate =
  len(vs) == len(ws) && roots_within(spine, mint_bound, vs, ws) && roots_within(spine, mint_bound, ws, vs)

fn roots_within(spine, mint_bound, vs, ws) with !Mutate =
  len(vs) == 0 || root_among(spine, mint_bound, row_root(spine, mint_bound, last(vs), 0), ws) && roots_within(spine, mint_bound, drop_last(vs), ws)

fn root_among(spine, mint_bound, r, ws) with !Mutate =
  len(ws) > 0 && (row_root(spine, mint_bound, last(ws), 0) == r || root_among(spine, mint_bound, r, drop_last(ws)))

// A root at or past the mint bound holds no cell at all (`graph_node_at`'s
// own guard): it reads unmarked and takes no mark, and the walk answers
// false at it one hop on.
fn marked(spine, mint_bound, r, stamp) with Memory =
  r < mint_bound && spine_mark_at(spine, r) == stamp

fn mark(spine, mint_bound, r, stamp) with Memory =
  if r < mint_bound { spine_put_mark(spine, r, stamp) } else { () }

// The loops walk a list from its last element back: an argument list is a
// snoc chain more often than not, and `last`/`drop_last` read it where it
// stands, where flattening it first copied it.
fn occurs_in_params(spine, mint_bound, handle, params, stamp) with !Mutate =
  len(params) > 0 && (occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, param_ty(last(params)), stamp) || occurs_in_params(spine, mint_bound, handle, drop_last(params), stamp))

fn occurs_in_list(spine, mint_bound, handle, tys, stamp) with !Mutate =
  len(tys) > 0 && (occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, last(tys), stamp) || occurs_in_list(spine, mint_bound, handle, drop_last(tys), stamp))

fn occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, fields, stamp) with !Mutate = len(fields) > 0 && {
  let occ = match last(fields) {
    (_, ty) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ty, stamp),
  }
  occ || occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, drop_last(fields), stamp)
}

// The occurs-check twin of free_in_row: does `handle` appear in the row's
// parameterized-effect payload types (Iterate(a) carries a)? subst_ty freshens
// these payloads via subst_row, so the check MUST see them or a var bound to a
// type carrying itself in a row (result() -> r) escapes the check and subst_ty
// loops. One traversal shape across free_in_row / subst_row / here — they read
// the SAME structure; drift between them IS the bug this closes.
//
// ONE triple, one walk: the names on both fields plus the tail var —
// the three vars-of-a-row walks (free/occurs/subst) agree by having
// one shape to agree about.
fn occurs_in_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, eff, stamp) with !Mutate = match eff {
  EfRow{present: p, absent: a, tail: t} => occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, p, stamp) || occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, a, stamp) || match t {
    EtOpen(vs) => // A BOUND edge hides structure the write would embed: subst_row's
    // merge arm traverses into the binding, so the cycle guard must
    // reach the same leaves through EVERY edge (the trio law over
    // rows — any edge occurring is the row occurring). Structural
    // walk — the mechanism layer's own discipline.
    occurs_in_edges(spine, mint_bound, handle, vs, stamp),
    _ => false,
  },
}

// Each tail cell's READ carries every payload its sources carried forward,
// so the check reads it once and never walks the sources — the walk that
// visited a hub once per path (341MB at one bind, 2026-09-21) has no row
// half left to do.
// A row reached twice answers once, like a type root — a row whose read holds
// an instance carrying that same row (a task that re-spawns itself) is a
// cycle to stop at, not an infinite type.
fn occurs_in_edges(spine, mint_bound, handle, vs: [Int], stamp) with !Mutate = len(vs) > 0 && {
  let r = row_root(spine, mint_bound, last(vs), 0)
  let here = !marked(spine, mint_bound, r, stamp) && {
    mark(spine, mint_bound, r, stamp)
    occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, row_cell_at(spine, mint_bound, r).full, stamp)
  }
  here || occurs_in_edges(spine, mint_bound, handle, drop_last(vs), stamp)
}

// The ROW-side half of the residual bind's occurs guard: a tail that
// continues at the very handle being bound is the row sort's own infinite
// type, and subst would walk it forever. The other two arms carry no
// handle, so there is nothing to close.
// The record row to its end: a row var's residual fields, then whatever its
// tail continues at. Two open rows meet at one fresh var and each continues
// into it, so a cycle can run through the CHAIN and never through a field —
// the walk has to follow the continuation, not stop at the first residual.
fn occurs_in_record_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, v, stamp) with !Mutate = {
  let vroot = chase_handle(spine, mint_bound, v, 0)
  v == handle || vroot == handle || !marked(spine, mint_bound, vroot, stamp) && {
    mark(spine, mint_bound, vroot, stamp)
    match chase_node(spine, mint_bound, vroot, 0) {
      GNode(NRecordRowBound{fields: rest, tail}, _) => occurs_in_fields(spine, mint_bound, handle, rest, stamp) || match tail {
        RowContinues(next) => occurs_in_record_row(spine, mint_bound, handle, next, stamp),
        RowClosed(_) => false,
      },
      GNode(NBound(bound), _) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, bound, stamp),
      _ => false,
    }
  }
}

fn record_row_tail_is(tail, handle) with Pure = match tail {
  RowClosed(_) => false,
  RowContinues(v) => v == handle,
}

// A record row's residual shown as the record it makes: closed where a
// closer proved it whole, open at the var it continues into. The refusals
// render through it, so a diagnostic names a record, never a node kind.
fn record_row_as_type(fields, tail) = match tail {
  RowClosed(_) => TRecord(fields),
  RowContinues(v) => TRecordOpen(fields, v),
}

fn occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, names, stamp) with !Mutate = len(names) > 0 && {
  let here = match last(names) {
    ENamed(_) => false,
    EPure => false,
    EParameterized{args} => occurs_in_eff_args(spine, mint_bound, handle, args, stamp),
  }
  here || occurs_in_eff_names(spine, mint_bound, handle, drop_last(names), stamp)
}

fn occurs_in_eff_args(spine, mint_bound, handle, args, stamp) with !Mutate = len(args) > 0 && {
  let here = match last(args) {
    EAInt(_) => false,
    EAFloat(_) => false,
    EAString(_) => false,
    EAType(ty) => occurs_in(spine, mint_bound, handle, ty, stamp),
    EARow(r) => occurs_in_edges(spine, mint_bound, handle, [r], stamp),
    EANode(_) => false,
    EAEffect(_) => // interned identities — no embedded Ty, nothing to occur in
    false,
    EACon(_) => false,
  }
  here || occurs_in_eff_args(spine, mint_bound, handle, drop_last(args), stamp)
}

// Trail buffer — flat array + length counter
// Mirrors the nodes buffer substrate (DESIGN Ch 4 Salsa 3.0 shape).
// Append via list_extend_to + list_set — O(1) amortized. Revert is
// a backward flat-array loop, one list_set per step — O(M) exact.
// Rollback resets trail_len; entries above the counter are stale
// and get overwritten on next append. No snoc, no concat, no linked
// walk. This is the substrate on which Mentl's oracle runs.
//
// The snapshot-path walker: undo the trail backward IN PLACE into the
// fork-time table. Pages never move, so every inverse lands on its live
// cell (backward order makes the oldest old-value per slot the pre-fork
// one); an entry whose band opened only during the fork is skipped by the
// open-count guard — its page dies with the region, and writing into it
// would resurrect the doomed-allocation class. MFreshNode is the UN-MINT:
// the checkpoint restores `next` wholesale, and the slot's cell goes back to
// empty — the node is overwritten at the re-mint, the cell is not, and a
// row cell left standing at a number the re-mint hands a type variable
// reads that variable as a row.
fn revert_trail_into(ref spine, spine_open, ref trail, current, target) with Memory = if current <= target {
  ()
} else {
  let idx = current - 1
  let mutation = trail[idx]
  match mutation {
    MSetNode(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_node(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target)
    },
    MSetRow(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_cell(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target)
    },
    MSetBoundary(h, old) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_boundary(spine, h, old) }
      revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target)
    },
    MFreshNode(h) => {
      if spine_band(h) < spine_open { spine_put_cell(spine, h, CEmpty) }
      revert_trail_into(spine, spine_open, trail, idx, target)
    },
  }
}

// The trail buffer is not `own`: list_extend_to borrows it and the
// (possibly fresh) owner returns — this fn finishes nothing (the
// relevant tier's first sweep).
fn trail_append(trail, trail_len, own mutation) =
  list_set(list_extend_to(trail, trail_len + 1), trail_len, mutation)

// Effect-name set ops (H3.1)
// merge_row routes through `name_set_union` from effects.mn — the
// row entries are EffName values, not strings. Smart constructors
// (mk_ef_closed, mk_ef_open) enforce sort invariant at construction,
// so no boundary canonicalize is needed. The local string-set
// adapters are gone — string-set primitives still serve own.mn and
// other linear-binding consumers.
//
// ── DAG-homed here (the import-truth sweep, 2026-07-23): each fn below
// moved from a module DOWNSTREAM of its users so every user reaches it
// through a forward import edge — the manifest tells the truth.
fn node_handle(node) = match node {
  N{handle: h} => h,
}

// The MINT span of a handle — the reverse read of the span_index weave
// (fresh_handle writes (span, handle) at every mint). A node's REASON span
// can be overwritten by a later bind (a hole's reason becomes its binder's
// "declared as ..."), so a patch target must read the weave, never the
// reason. O(n) scan at felt cadence (once per accepted action); the O(1)
// form is the handle-indexed span weave (the §5.O name-is-handle sibling).
// The module path enclosing a span: the NModule weave entry whose span
// contains this span. "" when no module wraps it (single-unit weave).
// DAG-homed here (oracle and cursor both read it; both import graph) —
// module-of-position is a LIVE projection of the NModule spans, never a
// per-mint side-ledger. The O(next) scan's O(1) destiny is the
// handle-indexed span weave (the §5.O name-is-handle sibling).
fn module_path_of_span(span) with GraphRead + !Mutate = {
  let snapshot = graph_snapshot()
  match snapshot {
    Graph(next, _spans) => scan_for_enclosing_module(span, 0, next),
  }
}

fn scan_for_enclosing_module(target, i, n) with GraphRead + !Mutate = if i >= n {
  ""
} else {
  match graph_node_body(i) {
    NModule(m) => if span_contains(m.span, target) { m.path } else { scan_for_enclosing_module(target, i + 1, n) },
    _ => scan_for_enclosing_module(target, i + 1, n),
  }
}

// Every module in this weave, in weave order — the same NModule cells
// module_path_of_span narrows by containment, read whole instead.
//
// The DAG is a fact the driver PROVES on every invocation and then could not
// show anyone: answering "which modules does this entry pull?" meant a shell
// transitive-closure loop over `grep '^import'`, re-deriving by name what the
// graph holds by edge. That hand walk is what this projection retires, and it
// is a read of the weave, never a second walk of the filesystem.
// ONE CELL PER MODULE, keyed by PATH. The trial and the final each parse, so
// each mints its own NModule node for the same file — two cells, one module.
// Every consumer of this projection was therefore double-counting: the cost
// facet reported 107 modules and 119,128 source lines for a 53-module,
// 59,501-line wheel, exactly 2n+1 (the link root is minted once). A module's
// identity is what it RESOLVES TO, never the node that happens to represent
// it — the same law driver_collect_visit's visited set keys on, and the same
// one the census dedup needed. LAST wins, matching env_snapshot's dedup-to-
// latest for the same reason: the final generation is the judged one.
fn module_cells() with Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + !Mutate = match graph_snapshot() {
  Graph(next, _spans) => {
    let cells = {
      iterate_range(0, next)
      result()
    } ~> fold_handler(module_cell_join, [])
    dedup_module_cells(cells)
  },
}

// Keep the LAST cell for a path: the walk is handle-ascending, so the last
// cell is the FINAL generation's — the judged one, whose decls are the nodes
// the returned ast actually holds. A fold over the REVERSED cells keeps the
// first of each path it meets and the outer reverse restores mint order; the
// membership test is `any` over the kept cells' paths. (It was an
// index-threaded walk with a second index-threaded membership scan.)
fn dedup_module_cells(cells) with Memory + Alloc =
  cells |> reverse |> fold([], dedup_cell_join) |> reverse

fn dedup_cell_join(acc, c) with Memory + Alloc =
  if any({ a => cell_path(a) == cell_path(c) }, acc) { acc } else { push(acc, c) }

// THE CELL'S PROJECTIONS — one home each, a field read on the ANNOTATED
// receiver. A reader wants one field, and SYNTAX's pun for that is
// `{path, ...}`; but an open-row record pattern's offsets are provable only
// when the receiver's whole field set is known, and at an interior site — a
// lambda's parameter under `any`/`find` — the pattern's row variable is
// never unioned with the element type
// (`Hβ.infer.record-row-vars-are-not-unioned`), so the emit finds an offset
// from the pattern's own set and reads a NEIGHBOURING field. Measured
// 2026-09-21 on the first cut of this record: fourteen `{path, ...}` sites,
// and the board's own `record pattern(s) over an open-row receiver — within
// 0` refused them; a second cut bound every field in a closed pattern over a
// bare parameter and the same bound refused those four too — the receiver
// was still a variable at the read. The Intent Boundary `c: ModuleCell` is
// what the bound's own comment prescribes, and with it the read is a plain
// field access on a known nominal type. The pun returns when the union
// lands; it is the lathe that is behind, not the spec.
fn cell_path(c: ModuleCell) with Pure = c.path

fn cell_decls(c: ModuleCell) with Pure = c.decls

fn cell_span(c: ModuleCell) with Pure = c.span

fn cell_handle(c: ModuleCell) with Pure = c.handle

// Every NModule node in the weave, as a RECORD: `{decls, handle, path, span}`
// — the module node's own `{decls, path, span}` with its handle spread in.
// A reader puns the fields it wants (`{path, ...}`, `{path, decls, ...}`);
// nothing is read by position, and nothing is discarded with a `_` (the
// four-tuple this replaced was destructured `(_h, p, _s, _d)` at nine sites,
// which is position-as-identity — SYNTAX §«Labeled call arguments»: the
// field's NAME is the key, the order a projection).
//
// The HANDLE rides because it is the module's identity and the other three
// are projections of the node it names — the walk used to drop it, which
// driver_module_ast's own comment had already confessed ("discards the
// minted handle — reading the node instead of re-filtering is the O(1) form
// once the mint keeps an edge to it"). Its
// first consumer is `module_handle_of_path`, which is what lets an address
// name a module by the edge rather than by containment: spans are per-module
// 1-based, so a line number alone is not an address, and the caret projection
// was answering `src/synth_proposer.mn:733` with the lexer's scan_number
// from `src/lexer.mn`.
//
// The cell's DECLS ride out with its path and span. Dropping them made
// every consumer that wanted a module's own content re-derive it from
// text — which is how 52 dead imports sat unseen: a grep cannot tell a
// used name from one named in a comment, and the graph can.
// The cell is NOMINAL — a brand over the module node's own three fields plus
// its handle — so a reader's parameter can be annotated `c: ModuleCell` and
// every field read on it is provable (SYNTAX §«Nominal record types»).
type ModuleCell = {decls: [Node], handle: Int, path: String, span: Span}

// One fold over the handle range, total over the node kinds by enumeration:
// a seventh kind is the reader's decision, never a silent skip.
fn module_cell_join(acc, h) with Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + !Mutate = match graph_node_body(h) {
  NModule(m) => push(acc, ModuleCell{decls: m.decls, handle: h, path: m.path, span: m.span}),
  NExpr(_) | NStmt(_) | NPat(_) | NHole(_) | NTypeAnn(_) => acc,
}

fn module_paths() with Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + !Mutate = map(cell_path, module_cells())

// The module handle a path names — the inverse of `module_name_of_handle`,
// read from the same deduped cells, so it lands on the JUDGED generation's
// node rather than the trial's shadow. `None` when the weave holds no such
// module: the caller refuses rather than guessing, and the answer is an
// Option rather than a 0 sentinel because the link root is itself a module
// node and 0 is a handle it can legitimately hold.
//
// This is what makes `mentl <file>:<line>` an address at all. A node's module
// is stamped at its mint (`spine_put_module`, read back by `graph_module_of`),
// so naming the file resolves ONCE here and every downstream comparison is a
// word compare against that handle — never a path re-matched per candidate,
// and never span CONTAINMENT, which stopped being able to answer the question
// the moment each module's spans became its own 1-based coordinates.
// LAST wins, matching `module_cells`' own dedup-to-latest: the cells arrive in
// mint order and the final generation is the judged one.
fn module_handle_of_path(path: String) with Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + !Mutate =
  fold(None, module_handle_pick(path), module_cells())

fn module_handle_pick(path: String, acc, c) with Memory + Alloc =
  if cell_path(c) == path { Some(cell_handle(c)) } else { acc }

// The path a module HANDLE names — `module_handle_of_path` read the other
// way, over the same cells, so the two directions cannot disagree.
fn module_path_of_handle(mh) with Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + !Mutate =
  fold("", module_path_pick(mh), module_cells())

// `mh: Int` is the Intent Boundary carrying the eq PROOF, not decoration:
// the fold's accumulator leaves `mh` a bare variable at emit, so `h == mh`
// falls to a one-word compare and `T_EqTypeUnprovable` says so — right for
// a handle by luck, an address lie by construction. The sibling
// `module_handle_pick` annotates `path: String` for the same reason.
fn module_path_pick(mh: Int, acc, c) with Memory =
  if cell_handle(c) == mh { cell_path(c) } else { acc }

// ─── Import reachability — can module A see module B's names? ────────
//
// THE ONE HOME for a question two readers ask of the same edges: the
// cursor's proximity rank (is the target's module depended on by the
// caret's?) and the comment-reference gate (may this prose name that
// decl?). It lived in cursor.mn, which imports infer, so the gate could
// not reach it — and a gate that cannot ask the question answers the
// wrong one: resolution fell back to the whole link's FLATTENED env,
// where a name resolves whether or not the module writing it can reach
// that name. Measured 2026-09-20: 0 unresolved on the entry link against
// 57 per module. It is homed here beside `module_path_of_span` for the
// reason that comment already gives — graph.mn is the DAG bottom, so
// every reader imports it.
//
// A HANDLE-facing entry, because that is what callers hold: a node's
// module is stamped at its mint, so both sides arrive as handles and the
// same-module case is one word compare with no path materialized at all.
fn module_reaches(from_mh, target_mh) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory + !Mutate = if from_mh == target_mh {
  true
} else {
  module_imports(module_path_of_handle(from_mh), module_path_of_handle(target_mh), [])
}

// Does module `from` import `target` (transitively)? Reads the live
// ImportStmt edges in `from`'s NModule decls; the `seen` accumulator
// breaks import cycles. The module graph IS graph content — no side-ledger.
fn module_imports(from, target, seen) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory + !Mutate = if from == "" || contains_path(seen, from) {
  false
} else {
  let direct = module_import_paths(from)
  if contains_path(direct, target) { true } else { any_imports(direct, target, [from] ++ seen) }
}

fn any_imports(paths, target, seen) with GraphRead + Alloc + Memory + !Mutate = if len(paths) == 0 {
  false
} else if module_imports(last(paths), target, seen) {
  true
} else {
  any_imports(drop_last(paths), target, seen)
}

fn contains_path(paths, target) with Memory + Alloc = if len(paths) == 0 {
  false
} else if last(paths) == target {
  true
} else {
  contains_path(drop_last(paths), target)
}

// The direct import paths declared in module `path`'s NModule decls.
fn module_import_paths(path) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory = {
  let snapshot = graph_snapshot()
  match snapshot {
    Graph(next, _spans) => scan_module_imports(path, 0, next),
  }
}

fn scan_module_imports(path, i, n) with GraphRead + !Mutate + Alloc + Memory = if i >= n {
  []
} else {
  match graph_node_body(i) {
    NModule(m) => if m.path == path { import_paths_of_decls(m.decls) } else { scan_module_imports(path, i + 1, n) },
    _ => scan_module_imports(path, i + 1, n),
  }
}

fn import_paths_of_decls(decls) with Alloc + Memory = decls |> filter_map_imports

fn filter_map_imports(decls) with Alloc + Memory = if len(decls) == 0 {
  []
} else {
  let head_imports = match node_body_of(last(decls)) {
    NStmt(ImportStmt(p)) => [p],
    _ => [],
  }
  head_imports ++ filter_map_imports(drop_last(decls))
}

fn node_body_of(node) with Pure = match node {
  N{body} => body,
}

// The total source the medium processed for this entry — the SUM of every
// module's own extent. A graph read, so it is DETERMINISTIC: unlike a wall
// clock it can be ratcheted, and a landing that makes the medium process more
// source to answer the same question is a refusal rather than a number
// somebody notices later.
//
// IT WAS A MAX, and the max was right exactly as long as spans were offsets
// into ONE concatenated weave: the last line any module reached WAS the total,
// because the modules were laid end to end. The per-module parse makes every
// file start at line 1, so the max became the LONGEST MODULE — this answered
// 9929 (infer.mn's own length) for the whole 59,501-line wheel, and for two
// different entries whose only shared module was that one. A ratchet reading
// too small does not go red; it goes BLIND, and accepts every rise beneath the
// number it now reports (§11 tripwire 4). Summing the per-module extents is
// what "total source" meant all along; it is only now that the two differ.
fn weave_line_extent() with Memory + Alloc + GraphRead + !Mutate = fold(
  0,
  (acc, c) => match cell_span(c) {
    Span{start_line: sl, end_line: el} => acc + (el - sl),
  },
  module_cells()
)

// The birth-span read is O(1) BY HANDLE — the spans spine column
// (§5.O layer 2 landed at this read). The scan it replaces rode the
// generic find, whose iterate FLATTENS the whole (span, handle) log per
// call: the field projection calls this once per position, and a
// wheel-scale field exhausted the 4GB image inside alloc (measured:
// alloc <- flat_raw <- iterate <- find <- parse_span_of); the
// allocation-free scan then measured O(n^2) over the snoc-spined log
// (a 600s timeout at the same scale). The column is the same one write
// projected by handle — the log survives for containment scans.
fn parse_span_of(handle) with GraphRead = graph_span_of(handle)

// The ONLY route from a TypeHandle to a resolved Ty. Delegates to
// graph_chase. No cache. No staleness. Every observer reads live.
// The effect decl beside its ONE handler (the order-independence law).
effect LookupTy {
  lookup_ty(Int) -> Ty
  // The specialization bracket (named-generic twins): while set, the handler
  // answers a free var through the site's EXACT instantiation pairs (root
  // handle -> concrete type; sound since the subscript fracture fix made a
  // generic body's element classes one union-find root), and the
  // active enc lets the closure-mint arm redirect a nested lambda to its
  // twin record. State on the ONE LookupTy handler — never a second handler
  // (singleton tier).
  spec_ctx_set([(Int, Ty)], String)
  spec_ctx_clear()
  spec_enc_active() -> String
}

// ─── the specialization projection — lookup_ty under a twin's pairs ──
//
// The key is the repr digit vector over the scheme's quantified vars, sorted
// by root handle — tiny, and maximally shared: every word instantiation IS
// the floor emission ([Int], String, records — byte-identical, Law 7); only
// a genuinely wide var mints a twin ($min$sp2 = min with its one var at f64).
fn spec_pairs_find(pairs, root) with Memory + Alloc = match find({ (v, _) => v == root }, pairs) {
  Some(p) => match p {
    (_, t) => Some(t),
  },
  None => None,
}

// Exact root-keyed substitution — the twin's one rule. Sound since the
// subscript fracture fix (Force = UNIFY at IndexExpr): a generic body's
// element classes and the scheme's quantified vars are ONE union-find root,
// so a free leaf answers its OWN root's binding in the site's instantiation
// pairs — and a free var whose root is NOT in the pairs stays the honest
// floor TVar. This is the precision the interim free-leaf rule (every free =
// the one wide type) could not have, and it lifts the one-wide-type demand
// guard: multi-type generics (a := Float, b := Int) twin correctly.
fn spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match spec_pairs_find(pairs, graph_chase_handle(u)) {
  Some(c) => c,
  None => TVar(u),
}

// A record row under a site's pairs, read to its END. The row var a body
// holds may be bound to a residual that continues at the var the scheme
// quantified — a rest binding, a second field read — and the site paired
// only that end. Looking up the first var alone found no pair and left the
// row open inside the twin, where the layout then had nothing to read.
fn spec_record_row(known, v, pairs) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = {
  let r = graph_chase_handle(v)
  match spec_pairs_find(pairs, r) {
    Some(TRecord(rem)) => TRecord(field_canonicalize(fields_present(known ++ rem))),
    Some(other) => other,
    None => match graph_chase(r) {
      GNode(NRecordRowBound{fields: more, tail: RowContinues(next)}, _) => spec_record_row(known ++ map({ (n, ft) => (n, spec_subst_pairs(ft, pairs)) }, more), next, pairs),
      GNode(NRecordRowBound{fields: more, tail: RowClosed(_)}, _) => TRecord(field_canonicalize(fields_present(known ++ map({ (n, ft) => (n, spec_subst_pairs(ft, pairs)) }, more)))),
      _ => mk_record_open(known, v),
    },
  }
}

// A record's fields with every PROVEN-ABSENT slot dropped: absence is a fact
// about the type, never a slot in the layout.
fn fields_present(fields) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc =
  filter({ (_, ft) => !slot_is_absent(ft) }, fields)

fn slot_is_absent(ty) with GraphRead = match ty {
  TVar(h) => match graph_chase(h) {
    GNode(NAbsent, _) => true,
    _ => false,
  },
  _ => false,
}

fn spec_subst_pairs(t, pairs) with GraphRead + Memory + Alloc = match t {
  TVar(u) => match graph_chase(u) {
    GNode(kind, _r) => match kind {
      NBound(t2) => spec_subst_pairs(t2, pairs),
      NFree(_) => spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs),
      NMaybeFree(_) => spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs),
      NAbsent => // a proven absence is not a var the site instantiates; the layout readers drop its field
      t,
      NErrorHole(_) => spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs),
      NRowBound(_) => // an EFFECT row — not in the pair space, which keys value shapes
      t,
      NRecordRowBound{} => // A RECORD row var is a quantified var like any other, and the site that
      // closed it proved the whole field set. Returning it verbatim left the
      // substitution unable to answer for the one var whose binding decides
      // every field offset, so the TRecordOpen arm below had to work around it
      // — and an INTERIOR site, a call inside another twin's own body, went
      // through neither and read the decl's unlearned row again. Unpaired,
      // spec_pair_or_var answers TVar(u), which is exactly what these arms
      // returned; paired, it answers the record that was proven.
      spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs),
      NRowFree(_) => spec_pair_or_var(u, pairs),
    },
  },
  TList(e) => TList(spec_subst_pairs(e, pairs)),
  TTuple(es) => TTuple(map((x) => spec_subst_pairs(x, pairs), es)),
  TFun{params: ps, ret: r, row: eff} => TFun(
    map((p) => param_with_ty(p, spec_subst_pairs(param_ty(p), pairs)), ps),
    spec_subst_pairs(r, pairs),
    eff
  ),
  TName{name: n, args, rows} => TName(n, map({ x => spec_subst_pairs(x, pairs) }, args), rows),
  TRecord(fs) => TRecord(map({ (n, ft) => (n, spec_subst_pairs(ft, pairs)) }, fs)),
  TRecordOpen{fields: fs, rest: v} => // A row var is a var, and the site that closed it proved the WHOLE field
  // set. Substituting the known fields' types while keeping `v` verbatim
  // discarded that proof, and the discard is what the wrong-slot class was
  // made of: the receiver stayed open in the twin, so the offset came from
  // the known set's own ordering instead of the record's. Paired, the open
  // row resolves to the closed record it was proven to be, and every offset
  // downstream reads the full sorted set.
  // The pair holds the REMAINDER the site proved — what the row var itself
  // carries — so the record is the known fields beside it. Pairing the whole
  // site record was right only while every known field was one the site
  // also had; an update's result knows a field its base may lack, and the
  // whole-record pairing dropped it.
  spec_record_row(map({ (n, ft) => (n, spec_subst_pairs(ft, pairs)) }, fs), v, pairs),
  TRefined{base: b, pred: p} => TRefined(spec_subst_pairs(b, pairs), p),
  TAlias{name: n, inner: i} => TAlias(n, spec_subst_pairs(i, pairs)),
  TCont{input: i, answer: a, discipline: d, world: w} => TCont(spec_subst_pairs(i, pairs), spec_subst_pairs(a, pairs), d, w),
  _ => t,
}

// lookup_ty_graph — GraphRead → LookupTy bridge (spec 05)
// The ONLY LookupTy handler in Phase 1. Declares GraphRead
// (inherited from the outer graph_handler). Free and error-hole handles
// resume TVar(h) — the honest word floor every consumer reads; a width a
// site genuinely needs detonates at emit's loud floors, never here.
//
// The handler additionally carries the SPECIALIZATION state (spec_pairs): a
// named-generic twin's emission brackets itself with spec_ctx_set/clear, and
// while set, a free var answers through the site's EXACT instantiation pairs
// (spec_subst_pairs above — root-keyed; a free var whose root is not in
// the pairs stays the honest floor TVar). State-swap, NOT handler-swap:
// a second LookupTy handler would demote every lookup_ty perform out of the
// singleton direct-dispatch tier — measured as ev-scan faults at infer sites
// that never threaded evidence (they never needed to under the singleton).
handler lookup_ty_graph with spec_pairs = [], spec_enc = "" {
  spec_ctx_set(prs, e) => resume() with spec_pairs = prs, spec_enc = e,
  spec_ctx_clear() => resume() with spec_pairs = [], spec_enc = "",
  spec_enc_active() => resume(spec_enc),
  lookup_ty(h) => match graph_chase(h) {
    GNode(kind, reason) => if len(spec_pairs) > 0 {
      match kind {
        NBound(t) => resume(spec_subst_pairs(t, spec_pairs)),
        NFree(_) => resume(spec_pair_or_var(h, spec_pairs)),
        NMaybeFree(_) => resume(spec_pair_or_var(h, spec_pairs)),
        NAbsent => resume(TVar(h)),
        NErrorHole(_) => resume(spec_pair_or_var(h, spec_pairs)),
        NRowBound(_) => resume(TUnit),
        NRecordRowBound{} => resume(TUnit),
        NRowFree(_) => resume(TUnit),
      }
    } else {
      match kind {
        NBound(t) => resume(t),
        NErrorHole(_) => // An error hole / free var resumes TVar(h) — CARRY THE HANDLE. The
        // prior TName("ERROR_HOLE")/TName("UNRESOLVED") sentinels fabricated
        // a NOMINAL type for a node the graph proves is unresolved: every
        // downstream type-directed read then trusted the lie — the eq
        // dispatch minted a structural-sum helper $eq_nUNRESOLVED that read
        // a raw Int operand as a variant pointer (memory at address 44100 as
        // a tag word). TVar(h) is the truth: repr_of reads the word floor,
        // fold_sig reads "i", eq reads word identity — the honest
        // productive-under-error floor at every consumer, no second sentinel
        // channel. (A quantified var's == at a GENERIC body is word identity
        // by the same floor; the type-directed eq for a generic operand at
        // its instantiated width is the evidence-passing band —
        // Hβ.emit.generic-boundary-repr-coercion.)
        resume(TVar(h)),
        NFree(_) => // A free var read at lower is NOT an error — it is a legal quantified
        // parameter of a reachable generic declaration (generalize's Forall is
        // the graph's own proof; `fn ident(x) = x` called from main carries
        // one), and repr_of floors it to the honest word by design. The per-read
        // report here re-derived "free = error" against that proof — 182
        // false diagnostics on a clean runtime-lib compile, the whole
        // frontier-gate debt class. A GENUINELY stuck width still detonates
        // at emit's loud comment-marked floors (field-offset-unprovable, the
        // ft mismatch), at the site that actually needs the width — the
        // consumer owns the refusal, never the read.
        resume(TVar(h)),
        NMaybeFree(_) => // A presence cell carries its handle like a free var: the field-slot
        // readers ask its node whether the field is there.
        resume(TVar(h)),
        NAbsent => resume(TVar(h)),
        NRowBound(_) => resume(TUnit),
        NRecordRowBound{} => resume(TUnit),
        NRowFree(_) => resume(TUnit),
      }
    },
  },
}
